        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "A prominent example cited in baseline agrifood compliance literature involves a commercial food manufacturer attempting to fortify a multigrain bread dough recipe with microencapsulated omega-3 fish oil. When scaling the formulation, the manufacturer discovered that the abrasive mechanical action of the whole grains during industrial mixing ruptured the microcapsules and caused the dough mixture to form dense clumps that prevented thorough baking, requiring systematic experimentation across mixing speeds, baking durations, and oven temperature profiles. Peer-reviewed food engineering literature independently corroborates the underlying physicochemical uncertainty: spray-dried microencapsulated fish oil powders (typically utilizing gum arabic, maltodextrin, or whey protein wall matrices) are highly sensitive to mechanical shear and thermal degradation above 50°C, releasing unencapsulated surface oil that induces hydrophobic particle agglomeration, disrupts gluten disulfide cross-linking, reduces loaf specific volume, and accelerates hydroperoxide lipid oxidation unless mixing speeds, wall-to-core ratios, and baking time-temperature curves (such as comparing 150°C for 8 minutes against 160°C for 7 minutes) are systematically optimized."
