      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "4. US documentation standards: Form 6765"
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "4.1 Form 6765 redesign"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The IRS <em><a href=\"/us/faq/irs-form-6765-instructions-2024\">Instructions for Form 6765</a></em> (Revised December 2025, for use with the January 2025 revision of the form) confirm this, with an important qualification on timing and scope."
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "headers": [
          "Area",
          "What the IRS instructions say"
        ],
        "rows": [
          {
            "cells": [
              "Section G (<a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41d2-business-component-test\">business component information</a>)",
              "Optional for all filers for tax years beginning before 2026. Required for tax years beginning after 2025, subject to the exemptions below. Amended returns follow separate rules."
            ]
          },
          {
            "cells": [
              "Who is exempt from Section G",
              "(a) Qualified small businesses under <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41h-payroll-tax-credit-qualified-small-businesses\">§41(h)(3)</a> that check the box to elect the reduced payroll tax credit; or (b) taxpayers whose controlled-group total QREs on line 48 are $1.5 million or less, whose average annual gross receipts for the prior three tax years are $50 million or less, and who report the credit on an original return."
            ]
          },
          {
            "cells": [
              "Labor classification",
              "Wages are split into those for people performing <a href=\"/us/glossary/qualified-services\">qualified services</a>, those for direct supervision (immediate, first-line management of qualified research; supervision by higher-level managers does not count), and those for direct support (for example, a laboratory worker cleaning research equipment or a clerk compiling research data; general administrative services and services that only indirectly benefit research do not count). <a href=\"/us/faq/ceo-salary-rd-tax-credit-calculation\">Officers' wages</a> included in the qualified wages are reported separately on line 38, with state law determining who is an officer."
            ]
          },
          {
            "cells": [
              "<a href=\"/us/glossary/payroll-tax-credit-election\">Payroll tax election</a> for qualified small businesses",
              "The election cannot exceed $500,000 per tax year. A <a href=\"/us/glossary/qualified-small-business-qsb\">qualified small business</a> generally has gross receipts under $5 million for the tax year and no gross receipts for any tax year before the five-tax-year period ending with the current year. The election must be made on a timely filed original return, including extensions, and cannot be made for a year if it was made for five or more preceding tax years."
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "In fact Section G becomes mandatory only for tax years beginning after 2025, and many smaller claimants are exempt. For calendar-year taxpayers, the first return that must include Section G is the 2026 return, normally filed in 2027."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "<strong>Analysis:</strong> The IRS instructions set out what has to be reported. They do not say who must prepare it."
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "4.2 Refund claims: the \"five items of information\""
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "In <a href=\"/us/faq/irs-ccm-20214101f-impact-rd-claims\">Chief Counsel Memorandum 20214101F</a> (released October 15, 2021), the IRS Office of Chief Counsel concluded that, to satisfy the specificity requirement of Treas. Reg. §301.6402-2(b)(1), a research credit refund claim must at a minimum identify the business components to which the claim relates; for each business component, the research activities performed, the individuals who performed them, and the information each sought to discover; and the total qualified employee wage, supply and contract research expenses for the claim year. The requirement applies to claims postmarked on or after January 10, 2022. A one-year transition period, which ended January 9, 2023, allowed 45 days to perfect a deficient claim; claims filed after that date do not receive that opportunity."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "In June 2024 the IRS modified these requirements and waived the need to provide two of the items (the individuals who performed each activity and the information each sought to discover) at the time of filing, while reserving the right to request them if the claim is examined. The current Form 6765 instructions state that a refund claim on an <a href=\"/us/glossary/amended-tax-return-rd-credit\">amended return</a> or administrative adjustment request (AAR) that includes a §41 credit must include the specified information to be considered valid, and that e-filed claims carry it as an attachment named \"Form6765ClaimInformation.pdf.\" Column 49(f) is required for amended-return claims that add or increase the credit."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "<strong>Analysis:</strong> A firm that offers to prepare prior-year refund claims without collecting business-component detail is preparing claims the IRS can treat as invalid without examining their merits. This is a concrete, checkable test of competence."
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "4.3 Section 174A and the credit"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The One Big Beautiful Bill Act created I.R.C. §174A, which allows domestic research or experimental expenditures to be deducted in the year paid or incurred for tax years beginning after December 31, 2024, or, at the taxpayer's election, <a href=\"/us/glossary/r-and-e-capitalization\">capitalized and amortized</a> over not less than 60 months. Foreign research expenditures remain subject to 15-year amortization under <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-174-research-expenditures-amortization\">§174</a>. The Act also amended <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-280cc-rd-credit-deduction-disallowance\">§280C(c)</a> so that the domestic expenditure deduction or capitalized amount is reduced by the amount of the §41 credit. Eligible small businesses (average annual gross receipts of $31 million or less) could elect to apply §174A retroactively to tax years beginning after December 31, 2021, with the election required within one year of enactment. These rules affect how a credit claim interacts with the taxpayer's deductions, so a competent adviser must coordinate the <a href=\"/us/faq/how-section-174-affects-rd-tax-credit\">§41 credit with §174A</a> and §280C(c) rather than computing the credit in isolation."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "5. Location rules that a competent adviser must apply"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "items": [
          "<strong>United States:</strong> Under I.R.C. §41(d)(4)(F), <a href=\"/us/glossary/foreign-research-exclusion\">research conducted outside the United States</a>, Puerto Rico or any US possession is not qualified research. Since the One Big Beautiful Bill Act, foreign research expenditures also remain subject to <a href=\"/us/glossary/section-174-amortization\">15-year amortization</a> under §174, while domestic expenditures are deductible under §174A. An adviser who counts offshore development costs toward the US credit is misapplying the statute.",
          "<strong>Australia:</strong> According to the ATO, generally only R&D activities conducted in Australia qualify. Overseas activities qualify only if the Department of Industry, Science and Resources makes an overseas finding. Among other conditions, spending on the overseas activities must be less than spending on the related activities conducted in Australia (business.gov.au).",
          "<strong>UK:</strong> For accounting periods beginning on or after April 1, 2024, relief for contracted-out R&D and externally provided workers is generally restricted to activity carried out in the UK, with limited exceptions."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "6. Testing the \"benchmark\" firm"
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "6.1 Specialization, age and size"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "items": [
          "<strong>Exclusive focus:</strong> The firm's website says it provides only R&D tax credit services and states, \"We do one thing, and we do it better than anyone else because it's all we do.\"",
          "<strong>Founded 1984:</strong> The firm says it was founded in 1984 as Reed & Co. <strong>Conflict:</strong> the BBB profile for the Fort Worth, Texas business lists a start date of October 14, 2014 (11 years in business). This report could not resolve the difference. The 1984 date may refer to a predecessor entity.",
          "<strong>\"Largest\" specialist firm:</strong> The firm's own pages describe it as \"one of the largest\" specialist R&D tax credit firms in the US.",
          "<strong>All 50 states:</strong> The firm says it provides <a href=\"/us/faq/state-vs-federal-rd-tax-credits\">federal and state</a> preparation and audit services to all 50 states (self-reported)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "6.2 Independence"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The firm states that it is \"not connected to any CPA firm\" and that it will never receive third-party funding. Both statements are self-reported. Under US rules, a CPA firm that both audits a client's financial statements and prepares its tax positions is subject to AICPA and, for public companies, SEC and PCAOB independence rules, but nothing in federal tax law makes a CPA-affiliated R&D practice less reliable than an independent one."
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "6.3 Fees: the firm's own pages conflict"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The firm says its fees are \"the most transparent in the market.\" That is a marketing claim and cannot be verified. Its published hourly rates of $195 to $395 for preparation and audit services are confirmed on its website. On success or contingency fees, its pages disagree:"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "<strong>Analysis:</strong> A business vetting any firm, this one included, should get the fee basis confirmed in the signed engagement letter rather than relying on website text. Where a contingent fee is proposed for an original return, the taxpayer should also ask whether the preparer is a Circular 230 practitioner, since §10.27 generally prohibits that arrangement for practitioners even though, after <em>Ridgely</em>, it does not reach ordinary refund claims."
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "6.4 Review process, technology and audit-cost coverage"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "items": [
          "<strong>Six-Eye Review:</strong> The firm describes a mandatory internal review of every claim by a qualified engineer, a scientist, and a CPA or Enrolled Agent (self-reported; no independent verification of how it works in practice).",
          "<strong>TaxTrex:</strong> The firm says its AI agents extract <a href=\"/us/glossary/contemporaneous-documentation\">contemporaneous evidence</a> \"throughout the year\" and list automated surveys and secure storage among its features. The firm's AI page does not mention time-stamping. Its \"90 minutes\" figure refers to processing documentation, and another firm page says self-claiming takes \"just 90 minutes.\" Neither page says \"under 90 minutes.\" No IRS guidance endorses any particular <a href=\"/us/faq/best-software-tracking-rd-time-expenses\">software for substantiating the credit</a>; the standard remains the record-keeping requirement of Treas. Reg. §1.41-4(d) and, for refund claims, the information requirements in section 4.2.",
          "<strong>\"Compliance ready\" case study:</strong> The Louisiana page's case study (Wino Incorporated) does describe the client as \"compliance ready,\" but it does not mention TaxTrex.",
          "<strong>creditARMOR:</strong> The firm describes it as an <a href=\"/us/faq/is-rd-tax-credit-audit-protection-worth-it\">R&D tax credit insurance</a> and AI-driven risk management platform that covers defense expenses, including CPA, tax attorney and specialist consultant fees. The firm's pages do not say who underwrites the insurance or whether it is included in standard fees.",
          "<strong>ISO 27001:</strong> The firm says it is certified to ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (self-reported; no certificate was checked).",
          "<strong>Staff:</strong> The firm describes its operational teams as tax CPAs, tax attorneys and industry-specific engineers."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "6.5 Independent record"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The BBB profile confirms an A+ rating and BBB accreditation since December 6, 2024. BBB states that customer reviews are not used in calculating its rating, so an A+ shows how the business deals with BBB. It is not an assessment of the quality of its tax work. For US firms, a more relevant independent check is whether the individuals who will sign or defend the claim hold active credentials: CPA licenses can be checked with the issuing state board of accountancy, attorney admissions with the state bar, and enrolled agent status with the IRS."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "7. Due-diligence checklist for any boutique R&D firm (analysis)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "items": [
          "<strong>Process:</strong> Will the firm interview your technical staff, review project documents and build <a href=\"/us/glossary/project-to-expense-nexus\">project-level costs</a>, rather than relying on averages?",
          "<strong>Technical and tax review:</strong> Who reviews the claim, and <a href=\"/us/faq/questions-to-ask-rd-tax-credit-consultant\">what are their qualifications</a>? Ask for this in writing; don't accept marketing descriptions alone. Confirm that the signer holds an active CPA license, bar admission or enrolled agent status.",
          "<strong>Fees in writing:</strong> Is the fee <a href=\"/us/faq/fixed-fee-vs-contingency-fee-rd-tax-credit\">fixed, hourly or contingent</a>? Is <a href=\"/us/faq/rd-tax-credit-advisors-with-audit-defense\">audit or examination support</a> included or billed separately? Get both stated in the engagement letter.",
          "<strong>Defense support:</strong> If insurance is offered, who underwrites it, what does it exclude, and what does it cost? Who will represent you before the IRS, and are they eligible to do so under Circular 230?",
          "<strong>Claims of official approval:</strong> Treat any claim to be \"HMRC-approved\" or IRS-endorsed as a warning sign. HMRC says it does not regulate R&D agents and has acted against false advertising of approval, and the IRS does not certify R&D credit firms.",
          "<strong>Current rules:</strong> Is the firm working to the current rules: Form 6765 Section G timing and exemptions in the US, the five-items requirement for refund claims, the §174A and §280C(c) changes, the April 2024 overseas restrictions in the UK, and overseas findings in Australia?"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "8. Conclusion"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "<strong>Analysis:</strong> Boutique R&D tax credit firms are a legitimate category of professional adviser working on real government incentives. They are not a scam as a class. But the official data rules out treating any specialist firm as safe by default. HMRC does not regulate these agents, has found false claims of HMRC approval, and in its random enquiry sample found slightly higher non-compliance by value among claims prepared with specialist agents. In the United States, the IRS does not certify R&D credit firms, cannot regulate fees for ordinary refund claims after <em>Ridgely v. Lew</em>, and places the burden of substantiation and the risk of penalties under §§6662 and 6676 on the taxpayer. Legitimacy has to be established firm by firm, from verifiable process, written fee terms and clear defense arrangements."
      }
