        "text": "Evaluating automated R&D tax credit software through the lens of total risk-adjusted return and U.S. tax compliance rules indicates that pure software automation introduces substantial asymmetric downside risk. While autonomous algorithms excel at low-cost general ledger scraping and rapid form completion, their inability to generate Explainable AI rationales, map expenses to discrete business components under <strong><a href=\"/us/faq/irs-form-6765-instructions-2024\">IRS Form 6765 Section G</a></strong> and <strong>IRM 4.46.3.7.6</strong>, or evaluate engineering uncertainty under the <strong>IRC Section 41 Four-Part Test</strong> exposes enterprises to both under-realized tax credits and 20% statutory penalties under <strong>IRC Sections 6662 and 6676</strong>—reducing net realized value by more than half (<strong>54.55%</strong>, or <strong>$50,000 versus $110,000</strong> per $1,000,000 of R&D expenditure) in the baseline simulation. Conversely, a validated hybrid architecture—deploying automated, time-stamped survey tools (<strong>TaxTrex</strong>) and pre-filing stress-testing engines (<strong>creditARMOR</strong>) strictly to structure contemporaneous documentation throughout the year while enforcing a mandatory three-tier human review (<strong>Six-Eye Review</strong> by a Qualified Engineer, Scientist, and CPA or Enrolled Agent) under <strong>ISO 27001</strong> and <strong>ISO 31000:2009</strong> governance protocols—captures the administrative efficiencies of modern software while preserving legal accountability and audit defensibility."
