              "26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(1)(C); 26 CFR § 1.41-4(a)(5)(i), § 1.41-4(a)(6)",
              "<a href=\"/us/faq/substantially-all-rule-rd-tax-credit\">Substantially all</a>—defined quantitatively in 26 CFR § 1.41-4(a)(6) as 80% or more of a taxpayer's research activities for each discrete business component, measured on a cost or other consistently applied reasonable basis—must constitute elements of a structured process of experimentation. A compliant process of experimentation requires three demonstrable elements: (1) identifying the specific technical uncertainty regarding capability, method, or appropriate design, (2) identifying one or more technical alternatives capable of eliminating that uncertainty, and (3) conducting an evaluative process through modeling, simulation, systematic trial and error, or <a href=\"/us/glossary/prototype-development\">prototyping</a> to test and analyze those alternatives."
