        "text": "Under <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41d1-qualified-research-definition\">26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(1)</a> and 26 CFR § 1.41-4(a)(2), an agency's software engineering activities constitute Qualified Research Activities (QRAs) only when they satisfy four cumulative statutory tests applied separately to each discrete business component. First, under the Section 174 / Section 174A Test of § 41(d)(1)(A), expenditures must qualify as <a href=\"/us/glossary/specified-research-or-experimental-expenditures-sre\">specified research or experimental expenditures</a> under IRC § 174—and, for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024, as domestic research or experimental expenditures deductible under IRC § 174A(a) or amortizable ratably over at least 60 months under § 174A(c), as enacted by Public Law 119-21. Under Treasury Regulation § 1.174-2(a)(1), this threshold mandates that expenditures be incurred in connection with the taxpayer's trade or business and represent research and development costs in the \"experimental or laboratory sense\" directed toward eliminating uncertainty. Second, under the Discovering Technological Information Test of § 41(d)(1)(B)(i), the research must be undertaken for the purpose of discovering information that is technological in nature. Third, under the Business Component Test of § 41(d)(1)(B)(ii), the application of the research must be intended to be useful in the development of a new or improved business component of the taxpayer. Fourth, under the Process of Experimentation Test of § 41(d)(1)(C), substantially all of the research activities must constitute elements of a process of experimentation relating to a qualified purpose under IRC § 41(d)(3)(A)."
