        "text": "Under the first hypothetical scenario in New Jersey, where the modeled state benefit rate is 10% (r_state = 0.10), multiplying the $500,000 QRE base by 10% generates a gross transferable credit of $50,000 (C_gross = $50,000). Applying the baseline market discount rate of 10% (d_market = 0.10) yields a combined buyer/broker discount fee of $5,000, resulting in net cash proceeds to the seller of $45,000 (C_net = $45,000). Evaluating sensitivity across documentation quality demonstrates the direct financial impact of claim substantiation: if the claim is backed by audit-ready verification that compresses the market discount to the prime 5% tier (d_market = 0.05, or $0.95 on the dollar), the discount fee drops to $2,500, yielding net cash proceeds of $47,500. Conversely, if weaker documentation forces a 20% discount rate (d_market = 0.20, or $0.80 on the dollar, the statutory floor in New Jersey), the discount fee rises to $10,000, reducing net cash proceeds to $40,000."
