        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "<strong>What the IRS release says.</strong> In IR-2024-31, the IRS advises taxpayers to avoid return preparers who base their <a href=\"/us/faq/is-30-percent-rd-tax-credit-fee-too-high\">fees on a percentage of the refund</a>. That advice appears as a tip in the release, not in its list of red flags. The release's red flags section names two warning signs: a <a href=\"/us/faq/do-boutique-rd-advisors-sign-as-paid-preparer\">paid preparer who will not sign the return</a>, which the IRS says is a red flag that the preparer is likely not to be trusted and may be chasing quick profits through big refund promises or fees tied to refund size; and \"ghost preparers,\" who prepare a return but will not sign it as the paid preparer. The release also reminds taxpayers that anyone paid to prepare or help prepare federal returns must have a valid Preparer Tax Identification Number (PTIN) and must sign and include it on the return, and that preparer misconduct can be reported on Form 14157, Complaint: Tax Return Preparer."
