      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The taxpayer carries the liability for the return, and the advisor's competence and independence affect whether reliance on that advisor protects against the accuracy-related penalty and the penalty for excessive refund claims. Those two factors are therefore sound grounds for comparing R&D tax advisors. The $17 billion figure concerns conflicted IRA investment advice. There is no IRS advisor-\"disqualification\" mechanism; the governing standard is reasonable reliance under Treas. Reg. § 1.6664-4 and the case law built on it. The IRS's percentage-of-refund warning is a tip rather than one of its listed red flags, and the federal contingent-fee bar no longer applies to ordinary refund claims after <em>Ridgely v. Lew</em>. NASBA and BBB status do not certify tax methodology. A buyer comparing advisors should test each firm's claims against primary sources in the same way, and should confirm that any advisor it hires is ready for the Section G reporting that applies from the 2026 tax year."
      }
