              "The Tax Court held that the engineering consulting firm had not shown that the <a href=\"/us/glossary/irs-statistical-sampling\">sampled projects</a> involved qualified research, rejecting its assertions of uncertainty and finding that the record did not establish a process of experimentation; the court gave weight to documentation over testimony. Practitioner reports state that the accuracy-related penalties determined by the IRS were sustained. The holding is often characterized in commentary as a failure to identify specific uncertainties at the outset; that framing is commentary rather than a quotation from the opinion. The case also reflects the Tax Court's earlier refusal (order of August 29, 2023) to limit discovery to a handful of example projects. The lesson for CRM claims: generalized statements that \"integration was uncertain\" will not substitute for <a href=\"/us/faq/contemporaneous-documentation-rd-tax-credit\">contemporaneous, component-level evidence</a>."
