        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Special statutory and regulatory scrutiny also applies to <a href=\"/us/glossary/internal-use-software-ius\">Internal-Use Software (IUS)</a> under 26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(4)(E) and 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6). Research with respect to computer software that is developed by, or for the benefit of, the taxpayer primarily for the taxpayer's internal use (such as internal treasury accounting, human resources, or general and administrative enterprise software) is excluded unless the software meets one of the statutory exceptions or satisfies the additional three-part <a href=\"/us/glossary/high-threshold-of-innovation-test\">High-Threshold-of-Innovation (HTI) test</a>, which requires that the software be innovative in resulting in a substantial and economically significant reduction in cost or improvement in speed, involve significant economic risk where substantial resources are committed and technical risk prevents retrieval within a reasonable period, and be commercially unavailable for use without modifications that satisfy the first two prongs. Under the Form 6765 Instructions (Section G, Column 49(e)) and 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6), software is excepted from <a href=\"/us/faq/internal-use-software-rd-tax-credit\">Internal-Use Software treatment</a> if it is developed to be commercially sold, leased, licensed, or otherwise marketed to third parties; developed for use in an activity that itself constitutes qualified research or a production process meeting § 41(d)(1); or developed to enable the taxpayer to interact with third parties or to allow third parties to initiate functions or review data on the taxpayer's system without being used in any general and administrative function. Where software is presumed to be for internal use but also contains a third-party subset, it is classified as <a href=\"/us/glossary/dual-function-software\">Dual Function Software (DFS)</a>, allowing the taxpayer to isolate the third-party subset. Because decentralized protocols, smart contracts, Layer-1/Layer-2 networks, and custodial/non-custodial wallets are built specifically to allow external users and third-party nodes to initiate transactions, execute contract functions, and verify state data on the system, core Web3 protocol software generally qualifies as Non-IUS / Excepted software in Column 49(e). Finally, under 26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(4)(D), (F), (G), and (H), efficiency surveys, management functions, market research, routine data collection, routine quality control testing, research in the social sciences, arts, or humanities (including pure economic or financial tokenomics modeling), <a href=\"/us/glossary/funded-research\">research funded by any grant</a>, contract, or otherwise by another person or governmental entity (where the taxpayer does not retain substantial rights or does not bear financial risk), and any research conducted outside the United States or a U.S. territory are statutorily excluded."
