        "text": "Under 26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(2)(B) and 26 CFR § 1.41-2(c), <a href=\"/us/glossary/qualified-services\">\"qualified services\"</a> consist strictly of three functional categories that map directly onto Form 6765, Section G (Columns 50 through 52). The first functional category is engaging in qualified research (26 CFR § 1.41-2(c)(1); Form 6765, Section G, Column 50), which covers the actual hands-on conduct of qualified research. In Web3 organizations, this encompasses Blockchain Protocol Engineers, Distributed Systems Researchers, Cryptography Specialists, Smart Contract Developers, and Security/QA Engineers who are actively designing novel consensus protocols, coding ZKP circuits, writing and stress-testing smart contracts, or architecting source code security (including enterprise applications such as private permissioned blockchains securing electronic health records). The second functional category is <a href=\"/us/glossary/direct-supervision\">direct supervision</a> of qualified research (26 CFR § 1.41-2(c)(2); Form 6765, Section G, Column 51), which covers the immediate, first-line technical supervision of persons engaging in the actual conduct of qualified research, such as a Lead Protocol Architect who reviews pull requests, directs experimental test plans, and evaluates consensus simulation results, while excluding higher-level executive management that only approves budgets or sets general corporate strategy. The third functional category is <a href=\"/us/glossary/direct-support\">direct support</a> of qualified research (26 CFR § 1.41-2(c)(3); Form 6765, Section G, Column 52), which covers services in the direct support of persons engaging in the actual conduct of qualified research or persons directly supervising them. In decentralized systems, direct support includes DevOps and Infrastructure Engineers who configure, deploy, and manage dedicated testnet ledger clusters, isolated node networks, or continuous integration fuzzing pipelines used explicitly for testing and research experimentation, while excluding time spent maintaining commercial mainnet validators or general corporate IT."
