      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "VII. Strategic Synthesis and Compliance Recommendations for Web3 Taxpayers"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The convergence of heightened IRS examination enforcement on software and cryptocurrency claims, the mandatory phase-in of Form 6765 Section G business-component reporting for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025, and the enactment of IRC § 174A under Public Law 119-21 requires blockchain engineering organizations and their tax advisors to institutionalize four core compliance protocols."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "First, Chief Financial Officers and tax directors at Web3 enterprises must enforce granular chart-of-accounts segregation between capital hardware, off-premises cloud compute, and commercial mainnet operations to prevent the commingling of capital or operational costs with R&D expenses. All depreciable physical hardware—including ASICs, GPU clusters, and physical validator servers—must be routed to capital asset depreciation schedules under IRC § 167 and § 168 and excluded from Form 6765 Line 43 and Section 174A deductions. Simultaneously, DevOps teams must tag off-premises virtual cloud instances used for devnet/testnet consensus simulations, ZKP prover benchmarking, and smart contract fuzzing so that those costs can be isolated from commercial mainnet hosting and accurately reported under 26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(2)(A)(iii) on Form 6765, Line 44 and Section G, Column 55."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Second, protocol engineering teams must transition from <a href=\"/us/faq/reconstructing-rd-time-estimates-without-timesheets\">retrospective year-end wage estimates</a> to real-time prospective documentation aligned with Section G of Form 6765 and LAFA 20214101F. Because Section G is mandatory for taxable years beginning after 2025 (unless the taxpayer qualifies for the <a href=\"/us/faq/startup-rd-payroll-tax-offset\">QSB payroll offset</a> exception or the ≤ $1.5 million QRE and ≤ $50 million gross receipts original-return exception), organizations should map their Git repositories, pull requests, architectural decision records, and sprint tickets directly to discrete business components (BC-01, BC-02, etc.) throughout the year, capturing the exact functional wage split across direct research (Column 50), direct supervision (Column 51), and direct testnet support (Column 52) while documenting the specific information sought to be discovered (Column 49(f))."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Third, unincorporated DAOs conducting protocol R&D should proactively adopt formal statutory limited liability wrappers—such as a Wyoming DAO LLC, Wyoming DUNA, or Tennessee/Vermont DAO LLC—both to eliminate the default general partnership personal liability established in <em>Samuels v. Lido DAO</em> and <em>Houghton v. Leshner</em> and to establish a clear entity-level trade or business capable of claiming QREs under 26 CFR § 1.41-2(a)(4)(i). Furthermore, because foreign R&E expenditures are barred from the Section 41 credit under 26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(4)(F) and remain locked into mandatory <a href=\"/us/glossary/section-174-amortization\">15-year amortization</a> under 26 U.S.C. § 174 without abandonment loss recovery even after the enactment of IRC § 174A, Web3 organizations must verify and document the physical U.S. domestic location of all employees and third-party smart contract security auditors, ensuring all third-party research agreements are executed prior to performance, vest substantial rights in the taxpayer, and operate on a non-contingent fee basis."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Finally, for taxable years 2025 and 2026 onward, because the IRC § 280C(c)(2) reduced credit election must be made on an original timely filed return and cannot be made retroactively on an amended return, tax teams must model the Section 174A and Section 280C election trade-off prior to filing. Specifically, advisors must evaluate whether claiming 100% of the gross Section 41 credit (and reducing immediate domestic Section 174A R&E deductions by the full gross credit amount) or electing the 79% <a href=\"/us/glossary/reduced-research-credit\">reduced credit</a> under Section 280C (to preserve 100% of the Section 174A deduction, or electing 60-month amortization under § 174A(c) or 10-year recovery under § 59(e)) yields the superior after-tax cash flow outcome given the company's current federal and state tax bracket, Net Operating Loss posture, and Form 8974 payroll tax offset eligibility, while applying the manual Line 13 or Line 26 override required under the February 2026 Form 6765 instructions."
      }
