        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "(Historical Context Note on 26 CFR § 1.6109-2(d): Although the text of 26 CFR § 1.6109-2(d) promulgated in 2010 stated that to obtain a PTIN an individual had to be an attorney, CPA, Enrolled Agent, or \"registered tax return preparer\" authorized to practice under 31 U.S.C. § 330, the D.C. Circuit in <em>Loving v. IRS</em>, 742 F.3d 1013 (D.C. Cir. 2014)—discussed in <em>Ridgely v. Lew</em>, 55 F. Supp. 3d 89 (D.D.C. 2014)—invalidated the IRS's mandatory \"registered tax return preparer\" competency testing and licensing regime under 31 U.S.C. § 330, while leaving mandatory PTIN registration under 26 U.S.C. § 6109(a)(4) intact for anyone compensated for preparing all or substantially all of a federal tax return or claim for refund.)"
