        "text": "While 26 CFR § 1.6694-2(d)(3)(iii) permits a boutique R&D firm to use established forms or templates to explain the general operation of the IRC §§ 6662 and 6694 penalty and Form 8275 disclosure provisions, every specific return position in the R&D study for which there is a reasonable basis but not substantial authority (for example, a contested Dual Function Software [DFS] subset or Internal Use Software [IUS] <a href=\"/us/glossary/high-threshold-of-innovation-test\">High-Threshold-of-Innovation</a> position reported in Section G, Line 49(e) of Form 6765) must be explicitly identified and addressed, and the written advice must be particular to the taxpayer, tailored to the taxpayer's specific facts and circumstances, and saved in the boutique firm's permanent engagement file at the time the Form 6765 deliverable is transmitted to the client and the signing CPA. Furthermore, if the boutique advisor's study is intended to help the <em>taxpayer</em> establish a reasonable-cause and good-faith defense against taxpayer accuracy-related penalties under 26 U.S.C. § 6662 (or the 20 percent erroneous refund claim penalty under 26 U.S.C. § 6676), the study must also satisfy the three minimum standards of 26 CFR § 1.6664-4(c)(1): (i) the advice must be based upon all pertinent facts and circumstances and the law as it relates to them; (ii) the advice must not be based on unreasonable factual or legal assumptions or unreasonably rely on taxpayer representations, statements, findings, or projections known to be unlikely to be true; and (iii) if challenging a regulation's validity, the position must be adequately disclosed on Form 8275-R."
