        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "In United States tax practice, <a href=\"/us/faq/fixed-fee-vs-contingency-fee-rd-tax-credit\">contingent fee arrangements</a> are strictly regulated by <strong>Treasury Department Circular No. 230 (31 C.F.R. § 10.27)</strong>. Under 31 C.F.R. § 10.27(b)(1), a practitioner (including CPAs, attorneys, and Enrolled Agents practicing before the IRS) is generally prohibited from charging a <a href=\"/us/faq/standard-rd-tax-credit-contingency-fee\">contingent fee</a> for services rendered in connection with any matter before the Internal Revenue Service, including preparing or filing an original tax return. Moreover, AICPA Code of Professional Conduct Rule 1.510.001 bars CPAs from charging contingent fees for preparing original or amended tax returns or claims for a tax refund unless specific narrow regulatory exceptions apply. Avoiding percentage-based contingency fees aligns directly with the ethical standards of Circular 230 and reduces <a href=\"/us/faq/what-triggers-irs-rd-tax-credit-audit\">audit risk</a> associated with aggressive QRE inflation."
