        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Analysis of the governing United States and comparative international statutory frameworks confirms that businesses can be heavily fined for submitting an unsubstantiated, poorly documented, or inaccurate R&D tax credit claim. When an IRS or ATO examination disallows an R&D claim, the claimant faces not only the 100% clawback or forfeiture of the principal tax benefit and daily compounding interest (IRC § 6601 and IRC § 6622), but also tiered culpability penalties of 20% for negligence, substantial understatement, or erroneous refund claims (IRC § 6662(b)(1)–(2) and IRC § 6676(a)), 40% for gross valuation misstatements or nondisclosed noneconomic substance transactions (IRC § 6662(h) and § 6662(i)), or 75% for civil tax fraud (IRC § 6663(a)) in the United States, and 25% (lack of reasonable care), 50% (recklessness), or 75%+ (intentional disregard) in Australia. Furthermore, intense regulatory enforcement against scheme promoters—illustrated by U.S. promoter and preparer sanctions under IRC § 6700, IRC § 6701, and IRC § 6694 and Australian court-ordered civil penalties of $13.6 million and $22.68 million—demonstrates that engaging aggressive consultants exposes client corporations to immediate secondary audit sweeps."
