          "<strong>Systematic vs. Simple Trial and Error (Siemer Milling Co. v. Commissioner, T.C. Memo. 2019-37):</strong> In Siemer Milling, the U.S. Tax Court disallowed $238,670 in R&D tax credits ($122,424 for 2011 and $116,246 for 2012) claimed by a wheat flour miller across seven projects (including flour heat-treatment, wheat hybrid testing, and ozone integration) because the taxpayer failed to demonstrate a scientific process of experimentation. The court held that a valid process of experimentation \"must involve a methodical plan involving a series of trials to test a hypothesis, analyze the data, refine the hypothesis, and retest the hypothesis so that it constitutes experimentation in the scientific sense\". Unstructured adjustments or simple trial-and-error tinkering on the production floor do not qualify. However, the Tax Court in Siemer Milling also rejected the IRS's argument that an R&D project cannot span multiple tax years, holding that there is no statutory requirement that a taxpayer face a different technical uncertainty each year so long as technical uncertainty exists in the claim year.",
          "<strong>The 80% \"Substantially All\" Fraction and the Shrinking-Back Rule (Little Sandy Coal Co. v. Commissioner, T.C. Memo. 2021-15, aff'd, 62 F.4th 287, 7th Cir. 2023):</strong> Under 26 CFR § 1.41-4(a)(6), the numerator of the 80% fraction consists of research activities constituting elements of a process of experimentation, and the denominator consists of all research activities for the business component deductible under § 174 / § 174A and not excluded under <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41d4-rd-tax-credit-excluded-activities\">§ 41(d)(4)</a>. In Little Sandy Coal, the Seventh Circuit affirmed the disallowance of credits where the taxpayer relied on arbitrary, <a href=\"/us/faq/reconstructing-rd-time-estimates-without-timesheets\">unsubstantiated employee time estimates</a> and an \"all-or-nothing\" claim at the whole-product level without documenting subcomponents under the <strong>shrinking-back rule</strong> (26 CFR § 1.41-4(b)(2)). For a brewery or distillery, if an overall facility upgrade includes ordinary construction or routine equipment installation (which dilutes the process-of-experimentation ratio below 80%, as occurred in In the Matter of the Appeals of Walden), the tax specialist must apply the shrinking-back rule to isolate the specific experimental subcomponent—such as the custom inline DO sensor control loop or the experimental mash filter protocol.",
