      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "A generalist accountant examining a brewery's ledger sees line items for malted barley, hops, yeast cultures, silica gel filter aids, and aluminum cans, treating them uniformly as manufacturing inputs. A specialist trained in fermentation science recognizes when those same physical inputs were consumed in an iterative, hypothesis-driven experiment—such as testing four sequential yeast propagation pressure profiles to prevent fusel alcohol spikes, evaluating twelve boil temperature curves with cryo-separated lupulin to optimize alpha-acid isomerization, running silica xerogel and PVPP dosage matrices to eliminate chill haze at EBC 90°, or conducting 24-to-48-week sour beer storage trials across BPA-NI epoxy and acrylic can liners to stop aluminum leaching."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Contemporaneous Documentation, Form 6765 Section G, and Audit Resilience"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The procedural landscape governing R&D tax credit claims has tightened substantially following federal court decisions in United States v. Harper (847 Fed. App'x 408, 9th Cir. 2021) and Premier Tech, Inc. v. United States (No. 2:20-cv-890, D. Utah July 15, 2022), alongside the Tax Court's evidentiary rulings in Siemer Milling (2019), Little Sandy Coal (2021/2023), and George v. Commissioner (T.C. Memo. 2026-10). Under <strong>26 CFR § 1.41-4(d)</strong>, a taxpayer claiming a credit under Section 41 must <a href=\"/us/faq/documents-needed-for-rd-tax-credit-study\">retain records</a> in sufficiently usable form and detail to substantiate that the expenditures claimed are eligible for the credit."
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "Upfront Disclosure on Amended Returns and Form 6765 Section G (2026 Mandate)"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Breweries and distilleries must comply with two distinct tiers of upfront IRS reporting depending on whether the claim is filed on an original return or an <a href=\"/us/glossary/amended-tax-return-rd-credit\">amended return</a> (refund claim)."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "First, for Research Credit <a href=\"/us/faq/amending-past-tax-returns-for-missed-rd-credits\">Refund Claims on Amended Returns</a>, Treasury Regulation § 301.6402-2(b)(1) requires that a refund claim set forth in detail each ground upon which a credit or refund is claimed and facts sufficient to apprise the Commissioner of the exact basis thereof. Under IRS requirements (IR-2021-203, effective January 10, 2022, as updated on June 18, 2024 in FAQ 21), taxpayers filing an amended return to claim an R&D credit refund must submit three mandatory elements upfront at the time of filing: (1) identification of all business components to which the Section 41 research credit claim relates for that year; (2) for each business component, a description of all research activities performed; and (3) the total qualified employee wage expenses, total qualified supply expenses, and total qualified contract research expenses for the claim year (which may be reported on Form 6765). Two previously required upfront elements—the names of individuals who performed each research activity and the information each individual sought to discover—were waived at the time of filing effective June 18, 2024, though the IRS retains the authority to request them during an audit."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Second, for Original and Amended Returns under Updated Form 6765 Section G, the final updated <a href=\"/us/faq/irs-form-6765-instructions-2024\">Instructions for Form 6765</a> (Rev. December 2025, released February 6, 2026) establish that Section G (Business Component Information) was optional for tax years beginning before 2026 (unless filing an amended return) but becomes <strong>mandatory for tax years beginning on or after January 1, 2026</strong>. Under the <strong>\"80%/Top 50\" reporting rule</strong>, filers required to complete Section G must itemize their research projects by individual business component—classifying each in Column 49(d) as Product, Process, or All Others (formula, technique, invention, or software) and breaking down direct wages, supervision wages, support wages, supplies, computer rental, and contract research across Columns 50–56—covering a minimum of <strong>80% of total QREs</strong> reported on Line 48, up to a maximum of 50 business components (with any remaining components reported in aggregate by entering \"Aggregate Business Components\" in Column 49(c)). A brewery, cidery, or distillery is exempt from completing Section G for tax years beginning after 2025 if it qualifies for one of two exceptions: (a) the <strong>Qualified Small Business (QSB) Payroll Tax Election</strong> under <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41h-payroll-tax-credit-qualified-small-businesses\">26 U.S.C. § 41(h)(3)</a> (a corporation or partnership with under $5 million in current-year gross receipts and no gross receipts for any tax year before the 5-tax-year period ending with the current tax year that checks the box on Form 6765 Line 33a to apply up to $500,000 of its research credit against employer Social Security payroll taxes in Section D via Form 8974); or (b) the <strong>Small-Filer Exception</strong> for taxpayers with controlled-group QREs on Line 48 of $1.5 million or less, average annual gross receipts of $50 million or less for the prior three tax years (determined under § 448(c)(3)), and a research credit claimed on an <strong>original</strong> return."
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "Actionable Protocol for Brewery and Distillery R&D Substantiation"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "To build an audit-resilient technical record that satisfies both the Siemer Milling scientific experimentation standard and Form 6765 Section G <a href=\"/us/glossary/project-accounting\">project accounting</a>, brewery and distillery management should implement three operational controls:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "ordered": true,
        "items": [
          "<strong>Establish Project-Coded Scientific Batch and Lab Tracking:</strong> Assign a unique alphanumeric business-component identifier (matching Column 49(c) of <a href=\"/us/glossary/irs-form-6765\">Form 6765</a>) to every experimental recipe, fermentation kinetics trial, distillation cut study, filtration test, or canning line engineering project prior to execution. Link all <a href=\"/us/glossary/contemporaneous-documentation\">contemporaneous analytical data</a>—HS-GC-FID-FPD volatile ester and congener chromatograms, Competitive ELISA gluten results, EBC 90° and EBC 25° nephelometric turbidity logs, hemacytometer yeast viability counts, mash/wort pH and gravity attenuation curves, and inline DO/TPO packaging logs—directly to that project code, explicitly recording failed or scrapped batches.",
          "<strong>Document Hypotheses and Technical Uncertainties Contemporaneously:</strong> Before initiating pilot batches or equipment modifications, record a formal project brief defining the specific scientific or engineering uncertainty (capability, method, or design) and the testable hypothesis—for example, \"Trialing three temperature-pressure profiles in a cylindroconical fermenter to determine whether restricting initial wort DO2 and holding controlled fermentation temperatures suppresses ATF1 gene expression sufficiently to keep ethyl acetate below 18 mg/L while maintaining isoamyl acetate above 1.8 ppm\".",
          "<strong>Integrate Technical Personnel with Specialized R&D Tax Advisors:</strong> Mandate structured review sessions between head brewers, master distillers, QA microbiologists, packaging engineers, and specialized biochemical R&D tax advisors. Ensure <a href=\"/us/faq/how-to-track-rd-hours-for-tax-credits\">time-tracking records</a> cleanly separate experimental hours (direct research, first-line supervision, and hands-on support) from routine commercial production, verify that third-party lab and engineering contracts satisfy the three-part risk-and-rights test under 26 CFR § 1.41-2(e), and exclude non-qualifying general overheads and standard utilities under 26 CFR § 1.41-2(b)(2) prior to filing."
        ]
      }
