        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "In-house supply QREs under <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41b2-in-house-research-expenses\">26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(2)(A)(ii)</a> encompass non-depreciable tangible property used or consumed directly in performing qualified services, such as biological materials, cell lines, chemical reagents, assay kits, and experimental compounds. Explicitly excluded under 26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(2)(C) and the IRS QRE ATG are land, improvements to land, depreciable equipment, capital prototypes, overhead, license or royalty fees, travel, meals, telephone expenses, and general building utilities, unless the taxpayer proves <a href=\"/us/glossary/extraordinary-utilities\">\"extraordinary expenditures\" for utilities</a> required by specialized high-energy equipment such as laser or nuclear research under 26 CFR § 1.41-2(b)(2)(ii). Additionally, although frequently omitted from simplified three-item QRE summaries, 26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(2)(A)(iii) and 26 CFR § 1.41-2(b)(4) allow QRE treatment for amounts paid to another person for the right to use <a href=\"/us/faq/aws-cloud-hosting-costs-rd-tax-credit\">off-premises (time-sharing or cloud) computers</a> in the direct conduct of qualified research."
