        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Payments made to unrelated third-party contractors—most notably U.S.-based Clinical Research Organizations (CROs) hired to find, screen, evaluate, and enroll clinical trial patients or conduct bioanalytical testing—are eligible as Contract Research Expenses (CREs) at a general statutory inclusion rate of 65% under <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41b3-contract-research-expenses\">26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(3)(A)</a>. While <a href=\"/us/faq/contractor-expenses-65-percent-rule-rd-credit\">65% is the standard rate</a>, 26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(3)(C) provides a 75% rate for payments to qualified research consortia, and 26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(3)(D) provides a 100% rate for eligible energy research payments to universities, federal laboratories, or small businesses. Under 26 CFR § 1.41-2(e)(2)–(4) and 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(7)(iii), third-party CRO payments qualify only if five cumulative conditions are satisfied: first, the agreement must be entered into prior to the performance of the research; second, the research must be performed \"on behalf of\" the taxpayer, meaning the startup retains <a href=\"/us/glossary/substantial-rights\">substantial rights</a> to the research results (while secondary commentary often states the company must retain \"intellectual property rights to the findings,\" 26 CFR § 1.41-2(e)(3) clarifies that substantial rights need not be exclusive, provided the startup does not have to pay for the right to use the results under 26 CFR § 1.41-4A(d)(3)); third, the startup must bear the financial risk even if the research fails, meaning any payment contingent on success is disallowed; fourth, the work must be physically performed within the United States, Puerto Rico, or U.S. possessions; and fifth, any prepaid amounts may be claimed only in the taxable year in which the research is actually conducted under 26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(3)(B)."
