      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "5. State conformity to the 2025 changes and impact on 2024 balances"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "State corporate and personal income tax treatment of 2024 R&E expenditures and post-2024 unamortized balance recoveries depends on each state's method of conforming to the Internal Revenue Code:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "items": [
          "<strong>Rolling-conformity states (with selective decoupling):</strong> States with rolling IRC conformity automatically incorporate federal statutory amendments—including Section 174A and the Section 70302(f) transition rules—unless the state legislature enacts specific decoupling legislation. For example, <strong>Illinois</strong> operates as a rolling-conformity state under 35 ILCS 5/102. When the Illinois General Assembly enacted Senate Bill 1911 (<strong>Public Act 104-0453</strong>, signed December 12, 2025), Illinois specifically decoupled from the new federal 100% depreciation deduction for qualified production property under IRC Section 168(n) and modified its inclusion of net CFC tested income (NCTI), <strong>but did not decouple from IRC Section 174A or the Section 70302(f)(2) acceleration rules</strong>. Accordingly, Illinois conforms to federal Section 174A domestic expensing and the federal acceleration of unamortized 2022–2024 domestic R&E balances.",
          "<strong>New York State and New York City — Statutory Decoupling:</strong> Pursuant to the enacted <strong>2026–2027 New York State Budget</strong> and <strong>New York State Department of Taxation and Finance Important Notice N-26-1</strong> (issued June 16, 2026, <em>\"Reporting certain depreciation and research and experimental expenditures for tax year 2025\"</em>), New York State and New York City <strong>decoupled</strong> from the federal OBBBA amendments to IRC Sections 174 and 174A (as well as the Section 70302(f)(2) unamortized balance acceleration rules), retroactive to taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2025. For New York State and New York City tax purposes, taxpayers must add back federal deductions claimed under IRC Section 174A(a) and Public Law 119-21 Section 70302(f)(2), and must instead compute New York R&E amortization adjustments under decoupled state rules (requiring Separate New York modification reporting on 2025 and subsequent returns).",
          "<strong>Fixed-date (static) conformity states and pre-TCJA states — Nuanced Split:</strong> Approximately one-third of states enforce static IRC conformity tied to the Internal Revenue Code as of a specific date prior to July 4, 2025, meaning they do not automatically adopt Section 174A or the Section 70302(f) transition elections unless and until they update their statutory conformity dates.",
          "<em>States locked to TCJA §174 (post-2017, pre-July 4, 2025 conformity dates):</em> Fixed-date states whose IRC conformity date falls between December 22, 2017, and July 3, 2025 (and that have not enacted a specific R&E exception) continue to mandate 5-year domestic and 15-year foreign amortization for 2024 and 2025 expenditures and do not recognize the federal Section 70302(f)(2) 1-year or 2-year acceleration of 2022–2024 balances.",
          "<em>States following pre-TCJA §174 or state-specific expensing overrides:</em> Several major jurisdictions—most notably <strong>California</strong> (which generally conforms to the IRC as of January 1, 2015, under the California Revenue and Taxation Code), as well as states that enacted targeted R&E decoupling from TCJA Section 174 such as <strong>Georgia</strong>, <strong>Wisconsin</strong>, <strong>Indiana</strong>, and <strong>Tennessee</strong>, or states offering state-specific elections such as <strong>Alabama</strong> and <strong>Mississippi</strong>—never required mandatory 5-year TCJA capitalization for 2022–2024 domestic R&E in the first place. In those states, 2024 domestic R&E costs were already fully deducted on the 2024 state return, meaning there is no unamortized 2024 state balance to accelerate in 2025 or 2026 (requiring a state subtraction/addition modification to avoid double-deducting in 2025–2026 any 2022–2024 amounts accelerated on the federal return)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "6. Analysis: what this means for 2024 expenditures"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "items": [
          "<strong>2024 as the final mandatory domestic capitalization vintage:</strong> Domestic R&E and software development expenditures paid or incurred in calendar year 2024 represent the final annual vintage subject to mandatory 5-year TCJA capitalization and the 10% first-year midpoint convention. For calendar-year taxpayers that did not (or could not) amend 2024 under the small-business rule, 90% of their 2024 domestic R&E spend entered 2025 as an unamortized capital balance.",
          "<strong>Three recovery tracks for the remaining 90% of 2024 domestic costs:</strong> Under Section 70302(f)(2) and Revenue Procedure 2025-28, any taxpayer holding unamortized 2024 (and 2022–2023) domestic SRE balances can choose to (1) deduct 100% of the remaining unamortized balance in 2025, (2) deduct 50% in 2025 and 50% in 2026, or (3) leave the balance on its original 5-year schedule through 2029. Furthermore, under Revenue Procedure 2026-32, taxpayers filing Forms 3115 after September 4, 2026 to correct prior impermissible 2022–2024 domestic R&E methods must align any resulting net positive modified Section 481(a) adjustment with their elected 1-year or 2-year recovery schedule.",
          "<strong>Permanent lock-in for 2024 foreign R&E costs:</strong> Foreign R&E and foreign software development costs incurred in 2024 received zero legislative relief under Public Law 119-21. The remaining 96.67% unamortized balance at the end of 2024 must continue to be amortized ratably over its 15-year midpoint schedule through 2039, and under amended Section 174(d), abandoning or disposing of the underlying foreign research property after May 12, 2025 cannot generate a loss deduction or reduce the amount realized.",
          "<strong>Closure of the small-business retroactive window:</strong> For eligible small businesses meeting the $31,000,000 aggregated Section 448(c) gross receipts test, the statutory window to amend 2022, 2023, and 2024 returns to claim immediate Section 174A expensing closed on <strong>July 6, 2026</strong>. Small businesses that did not file amended returns (or qualify under the deemed-election or superseding-return rules of Revenue Procedure 2025-28) by July 6, 2026 must recover their unamortized 2022–2024 domestic balances prospectively using the Section 70302(f)(2) 1-year or 2-year acceleration options.",
          "<strong>Multi-track federal and state amortization ledgers:</strong> Because states are split across at least three distinct regimes—rolling-conformity states that follow federal Section 174A and 2025–2026 acceleration (e.g., Illinois), states that decoupled from Section 174A and federal acceleration in 2026 (e.g., New York State and New York City under Notice N-26-1), and states that either remained on pre-TCJA expensing throughout 2022–2024 (e.g., California, Georgia, Wisconsin) or remain frozen on TCJA 5-year amortization—taxpayers must maintain separate federal and state R&E basis and amortization sub-ledgers for their 2024 expenditures."
        ]
      }
