        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "While large taxpayers are limited to forward-looking recovery starting in their first taxable year beginning after December 31, 2024, Congress enacted special retroactive relief for qualifying small businesses. Under § 70302(f)(1) of Pub. L. 119-21, an \"eligible taxpayer\"—defined as any taxpayer (other than a tax shelter prohibited from using the cash method under IRC Section 448(a)(3)) that meets the gross receipts test of IRC Section 448(c) for its first taxable year beginning after December 31, 2024—may elect to have the amendments made by § 70302 (including immediate domestic expensing under Section 174A) apply retroactively to amounts paid or incurred in taxable years beginning after December 31, 2021. Under section 3.01(1) of Rev. Proc. 2025-28, an eligible small business making this retroactive election may either deduct its 2022–2024 domestic research and software development expenditures in the applicable taxable year paid or incurred under Section 174A(a) or charge them to capital account and amortize them over at least 60 months under Section 174A(c)."
