        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Maximizing R&D tax credit recovery in the interactive digital media sector requires aligning studio engineering workflows directly with statutory tax compliance. Video game studios must first enforce technical segregation at the <a href=\"/us/glossary/project-accounting\">project accounting</a> and business-component level, structuring internal tracking systems—such as Jira tickets, Git commit logs, sprint retrospectives, and time-tracking categories—to separate qualifying computer science experimentation, including custom rendering pipelines, adaptive AI/ML training, and low-latency server prediction, from excluded artistic asset creation and routine bug maintenance. This granular tracking is now a mandatory statutory filing requirement for most medium and large studios under <a href=\"/us/faq/irs-form-6765-instructions-2024\">Section G of IRS Form 6765</a> for tax years beginning after December 31, 2025. In parallel, legal and finance teams must audit <a href=\"/us/faq/outsourced-contractor-rd-tax-credit-eligibility\">third-party contractor agreements</a> prior to execution; because outsourced domestic engineering payments are restricted to a 65% QRE inclusion rate under IRC § 41(b)(3)(A) and require the studio to retain substantial intellectual property rights while bearing full economic risk under Treas. Reg. § 1.41-2(e), reviewing co-development and porting contracts before work begins—and separating domestic vs. foreign contractor pools under IRC § 41(d)(4)(F) and IRC § 174/174A—is essential to prevent the total disqualification of contractor QREs."
