        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Upon mandatory amended-return review and substantive examination of Scenario A, the IRS disallows the unsubstantiated 50% wage overstatement ($1,000,000 in wages) and the ineligible $260,000 contract research claim, reducing allowable QREs from $1,860,000 to $600,000 and allowable credits from $225,400 to $49,000. Under I.R.C. § 6676(b), the disallowed <strong>\"excessive amount\"</strong> is $225,400 − $49,000 = <strong>$176,400</strong>. Assessing the mandatory <strong>20% erroneous refund claim penalty</strong> under I.R.C. § 6676(a) on the excessive amount lacking a reasonable basis yields a statutory penalty of $176,400 × 0.20 = <strong>$35,280</strong>. Deducting the $35,280 penalty from the $49,000 allowable credit leaves a net retained tax benefit of <strong>$13,720</strong> (before accounting for internal executive time and external legal/accounting <a href=\"/us/faq/rd-tax-credit-audit-defense-cost\">defense costs</a>)—representing a <strong>72% reduction</strong> in net tax benefit compared to claiming the conservative, fully substantiated $49,000 credit from the outset:"
