        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "First, with respect to <strong>standard currency</strong>, according to the official Foreword of <strong>ISO 31000:2018(E)</strong> (<em>Risk management — Guidelines</em>, Second Edition, published in February 2018 by International Organization for Standardization Technical Committee ISO/TC 262, <em>Risk management</em>), the second edition officially <strong>canceled and replaced the first edition (ISO 31000:2009)</strong> following a technical revision that reviewed the principles of risk management, highlighted top management governance integration, and emphasized the iterative nature of risk management across open systems. Second, unlike ISO 27001 (which is a management systems <em>requirements</em> standard designed for accredited third-party certification), ISO 31000 is published by ISO as a <em>guidelines</em> standard (<em>Risk management — Guidelines</em>), meaning any third-party \"certification\" to ISO 31000 represents a private conformity attestation rather than an ISO-accredited management-system certification."
