      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Synthesis and Expert Conclusion"
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "What Triggers an R&D Tax Credit Audit"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "R&D tax credit audits are primarily initiated by statistical screening algorithms—specifically the IRS's Discriminant Function (DIF) system for corporate returns with total assets under $10 million (Activity Codes 203–217) and automatic LB&I Ogden Campus classification routing for corporate returns with total assets of $10 million or more (Activity Codes 219–230)—combined with random compliance sampling under the National Research Program (NRP) and targeted procedural filters. While filing Form 6765 on an original return is not an automatic audit trigger, audit probability rises sharply when a claim exhibits quantitative disproportionality (excessive QRE-to-wage or credit-to-revenue ratios, abrupt year-over-year spending spikes, base-period inconsistencies violating I.R.C. § 41(c)(5), or mismatches with general ledger and Form W-2 records), when a taxpayer files in a high-scrutiny sector such as manufacturing (which accounts for 39% of corporate claimant returns and 61% of corporate credit dollars per U.S. Treasury/IRS SOI data, and a 16.84% sector audit rate in the 2026 Boast.ai benchmark) or under an incongruous Principal Business Activity (PBA/NAICS) code, or when general return red flags (such as Schedule C hobby losses under I.R.C. § 183 or 100% business vehicle deductions) trigger an examination that cascades into the research credit. Above all, filing an amended return to claim an R&D refund guarantees upfront IRS scrutiny: under current IRS procedures (with transition rules extended through January 10, 2027), the IRS screens <strong>100% of amended R&D refund claims</strong> for specificity under 26 C.F.R. § 301.6402-2(b)(1) prior to audit initiation—a gatekeeping regime established by Chief Counsel Memorandum 20214101F after the Ninth Circuit held in <em>Harper v. United States</em> (2021) that conducting a substantive audit waives the government's specificity defense."
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "How Conservative Filing Lowers Audit Risk: Strategic and Quantitative Mitigation"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "A conservative filing methodology lowers audit risk by neutralizing the quantitative and behavioral anomalies that statistical screening models and IRS classifiers target. By engaging on hourly ($195–$395/hour) or fixed-fee structures rather than percentage-of-credit contingency fees, a conservative practice removes the financial conflict of interest that drives <a href=\"/us/faq/signs-of-aggressive-rd-tax-credit-advisor\">aggressive advisors</a> to inflate employee time allocations or bundle routine operational costs into QREs—and directly avoids the contingency-fee risk factor targeted by the IRS's Mandatory IDR and RCCATG guidelines. Simultaneously, proactively excluding activities barred by I.R.C. § 41(d)(3)(B) and § 41(d)(4)(A)–(H) (such as post-production tooling and troubleshooting, customer adaptations, <a href=\"/us/glossary/reverse-engineering-exclusion\">reverse engineering</a>, management functions, market research, foreign research, social sciences, and funded contract work lacking financial risk or substantial rights under 26 C.F.R. §§ 1.41-2(e) and 1.41-4A(d) as enforced in <em>Grigsby</em> and <em>Smith</em>) keeps claimed QREs within established industry norms, protects the 80% \"substantially all\" experimentation threshold enforced in <em>Little Sandy Coal</em>, and insulates the taxpayer from the 20% erroneous refund claim penalty under I.R.C. § 6676(a)."
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "How Conservative Filing Lowers Audit Risk: Structural and Procedural Mitigation"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Once a return is screened at intake or selected for examination, structural quality controls determine whether the credit is sustained. Integrating an ISO 31000-aligned risk management workflow (noting that ISO 31000:2018 superseded ISO 31000:2009) with an ISO 27001 information security framework standardizes contemporaneous data collection and project-level cost accounting from the start of the engagement. Subjecting every claim to a mandatory multi-disciplinary Six-Eye Review—combining independent engineering/scientific verification of the Four-Part Test with CPA or Enrolled Agent verification of financial tracing and tax law compliance—ensures that the resulting documentation package satisfies both the upfront business-component disclosure mandates of 26 C.F.R. § 301.6402-2(b)(1) and Form 6765 Section G (mandatory for non-exempt original returns for tax years beginning after 2025) and the substantive recordkeeping and project-to-expense nexus standards of 26 C.F.R. § 1.41-4(d)."
      }
