          "<strong>Modifying Existing Software for New Devices, Standards, or Vendor Platform Certification:</strong> Examining existing code or documentation, reverse engineering, debugging, and running target vendor validation or certification test suites to verify that software works according to standards or devices.",
          "<strong>Vendor Product Extensions:</strong> Writing customer code via well-defined vendor interfaces (\"user exits\") or Application Programming Interfaces (APIs) to fill functional gaps—such as custom spreadsheet calculations or custom report formats—where the vendor's core product is unmodified and the new code simply conforms to the vendor's API.",
          "<strong>Interface Software Development and Routine Data Conversion:</strong> Defining data and transaction requirements to connect internal or external applications or adding a web browser interface (<em>Interface software development</em>), as well as extracting data from a hierarchical database, converting data formats, and loading it into a relational database (<em>Data conversion</em>).",
          "<strong><a href=\"/us/glossary/reverse-engineering-exclusion\">Reverse Engineering</a>:</strong> Examining existing programs, databases, and files to determine how a legacy application or interface operates (explicitly excluded under IRC § 41(d) and 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(10), Example 8)."
