        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Second, labeling the X-axis \"Technological Uncertainty (Risk of Failure)\" requires nuance: while economic risk of failure is relevant to whether research is \"funded\" under IRC § 41(d)(4)(H) and 26 CFR § 1.41-4A(d)(1) (whether payment is contingent on the success of the research), technological uncertainty under 26 CFR § 1.41-4(a)(3)(i) and (a)(5)(i) exists even when the taxpayer has <strong>no uncertainty</strong> regarding its ultimate capability or method of achieving the result, so long as the <em>appropriate design</em> of that result is uncertain at the outset. Moreover, under 26 CFR § 1.41-4(a)(3)(ii), a taxpayer <a href=\"/us/faq/failed-development-rd-tax-credit-eligibility\">need not succeed</a> (or fail) in developing the component to qualify. Third, the only binding numerical threshold in Treasury regulations is the <strong>80% or more</strong> \"substantially all\" requirement under 26 CFR § 1.41-4(a)(6), which measures the proportion of a business component's research activities (on a cost or other consistently applied reasonable basis) that constitute elements of a process of experimentation."
