        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Average Annual Gross Receipts (AAGR) and related gross receipts metrics function as primary statutory gatekeepers and computational inputs for claiming the U.S. federal research and development (R&D) tax credit under Internal Revenue Code (IRC) Section 41. Rather than relying on a single static revenue figure, federal research credit compliance requires taxpayers to compute gross receipts across distinct statutory definitions, look-back windows, and entity-aggregation perimeters depending on the specific tax benefit claimed. First, under 26 U.S.C. § 38(c)(4)(B)(ii) and § 38(c)(5)(A), a non-publicly traded corporation, partnership, or sole proprietorship qualifies as an Eligible Small Business (ESB) permitted to offset the research credit against the Alternative Minimum Tax (AMT)—and, when paired with $1.5 million or less in controlled-group Qualified Research Expenses (QREs) on an original return, qualifies for an exemption from Form 6765 Section G business component reporting—only if its AAGR for the three preceding taxable years does not exceed $50,000,000. Second, under 26 U.S.C. § 41(h)(3)(A) and § 3111(f), a corporation or partnership (other than a tax-exempt organization under IRC § 501) qualifies as a <a href=\"/us/glossary/qualified-small-business-qsb\">Qualified Small Business (QSB)</a> eligible to elect up to $500,000 annually in R&D credits as a quarterly payroll tax offset only if its gross receipts for the current taxable year are strictly less than $5,000,000 and it had no gross receipts for any taxable year preceding the five-taxable-year period ending with the credit year. Third, under <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41c1-rd-tax-credit-base-amount\">26 U.S.C. § 41(c)(1)(B)</a>, a taxpayer computing the research credit under the Traditional (Regular) Method must multiply its Fixed-Base Percentage (FBP) by its AAGR for the four taxable years preceding the credit year to establish its statutory base amount."
