        "text": "Under Temp. Treas. Reg. § 1.448-1T(f)(2)(iv), gross receipts are recognized according to the taxpayer's federal income tax accounting method for the taxable year (determined without regard to § 1.448-1T) and encompass total sales net of returns and allowances, all amounts received for services, and all income from investments and incidental or outside sources—including interest (incorporating original issue discount and tax-exempt municipal bond interest under IRC § 103), dividends, rents, royalties, and annuities, regardless of whether those amounts are derived in the ordinary course of the taxpayer's trade or business. On a U.S. Corporation Income Tax Return (Form 1120), net operating sales after subtracting returns and allowances (Line 1b) from gross receipts or sales (Line 1a) appear on Line 1c, but a compliant gross receipts calculation must also incorporate investment and ancillary receipts reported across Line 4 (dividends and inclusions), Line 5 (interest), Line 6 (gross rents), Line 7 (gross royalties), Line 8 (capital gain net income), Line 9 (Form 4797 net gain), and Line 10 (other income), adjusted as required by the applicable regulation. Both regimes mandate reducing gross receipts by returns and allowances made during the taxable year (26 U.S.C. § 448(c)(3)(C); 26 U.S.C. § 41(c)(6); Temp. Treas. Reg. § 1.448-1T(f)(2)(iv); Treas. Reg. § 1.41-3(c)(2)(i)), and both exclude principal repayments of loans held by a commercial lender as well as state and local sales taxes legally imposed on the purchaser where the seller merely collects and remits the tax to the taxing authority."
