        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Third, although cross-border controlled group structures often raise questions regarding how foreign gross receipts are included in R&D credit calculations, existing statute, Treasury Regulations, and IRS instructions already establish a clear bright-line rule for foreign corporations. Under 26 U.S.C. § 41(c)(6), Treas. Reg. § 1.41-3(c)(3), and the Instructions for Form 6765 (explicitly stated in both the QSB \"Gross receipts\" definition section and the Line 6 and Line 7 instructions), in the case of a foreign corporation, only gross receipts that are effectively connected with the conduct of a trade or business within the United States, the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico, or any possession or territory of the United States (see IRC § 864(c)) are taken into account. Therefore, a foreign parent or sister corporation with purely foreign-source gross receipts that are not effectively connected with a U.S. or U.S.-territory trade or business does not have those foreign receipts included in gross receipts for Form 6765 purposes. Additional administrative guidance under IRC § 41(h) would, however, remain beneficial for foreign non-corporate entities (such as foreign partnerships) and for documenting the absence of U.S. effectively connected receipts across historical five-year look-back windows."
