        "text": "Treasury Regulation § 1.41-3(d)(1)–(2) illustrates two primary operational triggers of the <a href=\"/us/glossary/consistency-rule\">consistency rule</a>. First, under Example 1 of 26 CFR § 1.41-3(d)(2), addressing statutory or regulatory definition changes, if the <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41d1-qualified-research-definition\">statutory definition of qualified research</a> or QREs narrows or expands between the 1984–1988 base period and the current credit year, the taxpayer must recompute its 1984–1988 base-period QREs under the law in effect for the current credit year. In the regulation's example, Taxpayer X originally reported $720x of aggregate QREs across 1984 through 1988 ($100x in 1984, $120x in 1985, $150x in 1986, $180x in 1987, and $170x in 1988), but a post-1985 statutory narrowing of qualified research reduced its 1984 and 1985 eligible amounts under credit-year law to $80x and $100x; Taxpayer X must therefore adjust its aggregate base-period QREs downward to $680x when computing its FBP. Second, under Example 2 of 26 CFR § 1.41-3(d)(2), addressing changes in taxpayer claim positions, if a taxpayer claims a category of expenditure in the current credit year as a QRE that it did not previously treat as a QRE during its base years, the consistency rule obligates the taxpayer to include any comparable expenditures incurred during the base period when computing its FBP, and vice versa."
