      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Compliance Operations and Tax Policy Recommendations"
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "Operational Substantiation Protocols for Clinical Trial Sponsors"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Sponsors must systematically archive FDA IND submissions, master clinical trial protocols, protocol amendments, Informed Consent Forms (ICFs), and Institutional Review Board (IRB) correspondence, as these contemporaneous regulatory records establish the specific technical uncertainties present at the outset of each phase and <a href=\"/us/faq/documenting-process-of-experimentation-rd-credit\">document the systematic process of experimentation</a> used to evaluate alternatives. Simultaneously, enterprise accounting systems must deploy distinct <a href=\"/us/glossary/project-accounting\">project tracking codes</a> segregated by business component, clinical phase (Phase I, Phase II, Phase III, and Phase IV), and activity type, separating routine \"Standard of Care\" patient costs and general administrative site management from \"Investigational\" research costs directly tied to resolving technical uncertainty. Because domestic clinical trial costs are immediately deductible under IRC § 174A(a) and eligible for the IRC § 41 credit while foreign clinical trial costs are excluded from IRC § 41 under IRC § 41(d)(4)(F) and subject to 15-year amortization under amended IRC § 174, corporate tax departments must also maintain a strict domestic-versus-foreign split in their general ledgers throughout the year."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Prior to executing clinical trial agreements or work orders with CROs, research hospitals, or external analytics vendors, tax specialists must review contract terms to ensure the agreement is entered into prior to the performance of the research, the sponsor explicitly retains substantial rights to the research outputs, the sponsor bears the <a href=\"/us/glossary/significant-economic-risk\">economic risk</a> of failed experimentation, and vendor invoices segregate U.S.-performed experimental services from foreign trial sites, prepaid future-year research services, and non-qualifying pass-through expenses. Finally, when completing <a href=\"/us/glossary/irs-form-6765\">IRS Form 6765</a>, every filer reporting QREs must complete Section E (disclosing the number of business components, officer wages included, and any new expense categories) and, unless exempted via a Qualified Small Business payroll tax election or the small-claim threshold ($1.5 million or less in group-wide QREs and $50 million or less in three-year average gross receipts on an original return), must complete mandatory Section G reporting QREs by discrete business component (identifying wages, supplies, computer costs, and contract research for at least 80% of total QREs, up to 50 business components)."
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "Policy Recommendations and Post-2025 Legislative Status"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Because the current non-challenge posture rests on an internal administrative directive (LMSB-04-0404-024) rather than binding regulation, formal codification would insulate early-stage clinical R&D capital planning from shifts in administrative examination policy."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Updated administrative guidance would clearly distinguish qualifying experimental digital infrastructure—such as <a href=\"/us/faq/aws-cloud-hosting-costs-rd-tax-credit\">cloud-based computer leasing costs</a> under IRC § 41(b)(2)(A)(iii) and Treas. Reg. § 1.41-2(b)(4), tangible disposable biometric sensors under IRC § 41(b)(2)(A)(ii), and custom engineering vendor contracts under IRC § 41(b)(3)—from routine, general-purpose administrative IT and off-the-shelf software licenses."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "With domestic immediate expensing restored under IRC § 174A, ongoing tax planning for clinical trial sponsors now centers on modeling the interaction between IRC § 174A deductions and the <a href=\"/us/glossary/section-280c-election\">IRC § 280C(c)(2) reduced credit election</a>, alongside managing the 15-year amortization and disposition lock-in rules under amended IRC § 174 for foreign clinical trial operations."
      }
