        "text": "Commercial Availability operates as a decisive regulatory gatekeeper for taxpayers claiming the federal Research and Development (R&D) tax credit under Internal Revenue Code (IRC) § 41 for <a href=\"/us/glossary/internal-use-software-ius\">Internal Use Software (IUS)</a>. Under final Treasury Regulations promulgated in Treasury Decision (T.D.) 9786—published and effective on October 4, 2016—and codified at 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(vii), software developed primarily for internal general and administrative (G&A) functions must satisfy a three-pronged <a href=\"/us/glossary/high-threshold-of-innovation-test\">High Threshold of Innovation test</a>. Under this conjunctive standard, the taxpayer must establish that the software is innovative, that the software development involves <a href=\"/us/glossary/significant-economic-risk\">significant economic risk</a>, and that the software is not commercially available for use by the taxpayer without modifications that would satisfy the innovation and significant economic risk requirements. Pursuant to the effective and applicability date rules in 26 CFR § 1.41-4(e), while § 1.41-4 generally governs expenditures paid or incurred in taxable years ending on or after December 31, 2003, paragraph (c)(6) is applicable for taxable years beginning on or after October 4, 2016 (with transitional provisions in § 1.41-4(e) addressing taxable years ending before January 20, 2015, and taxable years that both end on or after January 20, 2015, and begin before October 4, 2016, although the full concluding text of those two transition clauses is truncated in the retrieved regulatory excerpts)."
