      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "C. Critical Compliance Pitfalls and IRS Audit Risks"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "During IRS examinations of research credit claims conducted under the <em>IRS Audit Techniques Guide: Credit for Increasing Research Activities IRC § 41</em>, examiners scrutinize recurring compliance failures across multi-entity taxpayers. The first compliance pitfall involves year-end membership testing, differing fiscal years, and intra-year acquisitions or dispositions. While December 31 is the statutory determination date for \"component member\" status of calendar-year corporations under IRC § 1563(b), crucial legal qualifications apply for R&D tax credit compliance. Specifically, IRC § 41(f)(5) cross-references IRC § 1563(a) (the controlled group definition) rather than IRC § 1563(b), and Treas. Reg. § 1.41-6(a)(1) tests whether a trade or business is a member of a controlled group \"at the end of its taxable year\" rather than on a fixed December 31 calendar date. For controlled group members with differing fiscal tax years, Treas. Reg. § 1.41-6(g)(1) provides that a computing member generally must treat the taxable year of another member that ends with or within the computing member's credit year as the credit year of that other member (subject to the anti-manipulation rule of Treas. Reg. § 1.41-6(g)(2)). When an entity experiences a short taxable year, it must apply the short-year proration rules of Treas. Reg. § 1.41-3(b) (multiplying or annualizing four-year prior gross receipts by months/12 for the Regular Credit) or Treas. Reg. § 1.41-9(c)(3) (multiplying or annualizing three-year prior QREs by days/365 for the ASC), alongside the fractional-year conventions for members entering or exiting a group. Furthermore, under the major acquisitions and dispositions <a href=\"/us/glossary/consistency-rule\">consistency rules</a> of IRC § 41(f)(3)(A) and (B), if a taxpayer acquires or disposes of the major portion of a trade or business (or the major portion of a separate unit thereof) after the base period commences, both the acquirer and the disposing taxpayer must adjust their historical base-period QREs and gross receipts (as well as three-year prior QREs under the ASC), including daily proration in the year of disposition under IRC § 41(f)(3)(B)(i), to include or exclude the historical amounts attributable to the transferred business unit. Thus, a late-year ownership restructuring cannot simply ignore the historical base period of an acquired or divested business unit."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The second major compliance pitfall is the omission of non-R&D entities from the controlled group base. The single most common audit adjustment identified in the IRS Research Credit Audit Techniques Guide is the failure to include commonly controlled entities that perform zero R&D, such as real estate holding companies, distribution entities with U.S. effectively connected gross receipts under IRC § 41(c)(7), or legacy service businesses owned by the same founders. Under the Regular Credit, omitting a sister company with substantial gross receipts in the four preceding years artificially depresses the group's base amount, invalidating the taxpayer's computation upon examination."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The third recurring compliance failure is the omission of family and option attribution tracing, compounded by improper treatment of intra-group transactions. IRS examiners routinely request complete capitalization tables, shareholder agreements, option ledgers, partnership K-1 schedules, and family relationship disclosures at the outset of an IRC § 41 examination—prior to evaluating the technical <a href=\"/us/faq/rd-tax-credit-four-part-test-explained\">four-part research qualification test</a> of IRC § 41(d). Unreported spousal ownership under IRC § 1563(e)(5), minor-child trusts under IRC § 1563(e)(6)(A), or &gt;50% parent and adult-child attribution under IRC § 1563(e)(6)(B) frequently reveals an undisclosed Brother-Sister group. Moreover, once a controlled group is identified, Treas. Reg. § 1.41-6(i)(1) requires transfers between members of the group to be generally disregarded. Under Treas. Reg. § 1.41-6(i)(2), if one group member performs qualified research on behalf of another member, the member performing the research includes its in-house research expenses in its own QREs (without treating intercompany reimbursements as \"<a href=\"/us/glossary/funded-research\">funded research</a>\"), while the member for whom the research is performed is barred from treating any part of the intercompany payment as a contract research expense. Similarly, under Treas. Reg. § 1.41-6(i)(4) and (5), intra-group lease payments for personal property owned by a group member are <a href=\"/us/faq/excluded-expenses-rd-tax-credit\">excluded from QREs</a>, and intercompany payments for supplies are taken into account only to the extent of the lesser of the amount paid or the other member's basis in the supplies."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "VII. Strategic Recommendations and Regulatory Developments"
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "A. Actionable Governance for Taxpayers and Practitioners"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "First, taxpayers managing multi-entity or closely held family structures must execute an annual constructive ownership and identical ownership mapping prior to closing the taxable year. This attribution matrix must cover direct equity, options and warrants under IRC § 1563(e)(1), upward entity attribution from ≥5% partnerships, trusts, and corporations under IRC § 1563(e)(2)–(4), spousal attribution under IRC § 1563(e)(5), minor-child attribution under IRC § 1563(e)(6)(A), and &gt;50% effective-control attribution for adult children, parents, grandparents, and grandchildren under IRC § 1563(e)(6)(B). Because the Brother-Sister effective control threshold turns on the narrow margin between 50.00% and 50.01% identical ownership, proactive modeling prior to year-end equity issuances or transfers empowers management to anticipate—or lawfully structure around—mandatory group aggregation."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Second, because both the Regular Credit (requiring 1984–1988 or start-up base years plus four prior years of gross receipts) and the ASC (requiring three prior years of QREs, which must be determined on a consistent basis under IRC § 41(c)(5) and Treas. Reg. § 1.41-9(c)(2)) depend on multi-year group histories, controlled groups should establish and maintain a permanent, centralized \"Base Period Book.\" This historical archive must contain auditable documentation of QREs and gross receipts for every current and former member of the controlled group, complete with IRC § 41(f)(3) acquisition and disposition reconciliations and pre-filing identification of the group's designated member under Treas. Reg. § 1.41-9(b)(4)(ii). Under IRC § 6001 and Treas. Reg. § 1.41-4(d), failure to substantiate base-period figures for a controlled group member can result in full disallowance of the Regular Credit or force recomputation under a less favorable method."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Third, where partnerships or LLCs are owned alongside corporate entities, practitioners must perform an annual operating agreement quantification for non-corporate entities, documenting both the capital interest and profits interest percentages of each common owner under Treas. Reg. § 1.52-1(c)–(d) and Treas. Reg. § 1.414(c)-2 and accounting for preferred returns, special allocations, and waterfall provisions."
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "B. Regulatory Developments and Form 6765 Controlled Group Reporting"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "While formal Treasury regulations under Treas. Reg. § 1.52-1 and § 1.414(c)-2 still lack granular safe-harbor formulas for variable partnership waterfalls—leaving that analytical recommendation relevant for future Treasury guidance—the IRS has directly implemented the <a href=\"/us/glossary/irs-form-6765\">Form 6765</a> controlled group disclosure mandate. On revised <a href=\"/us/faq/irs-form-6765-instructions-2024\">Form 6765 and its updated instructions</a>, the IRS introduced explicit front-page header items alongside Section E (<em>Other Information</em>), Section F (<em>Qualified Research Expenses Summary</em>), and Section G (<em>Business Component Information</em>) reporting requirements. At the top of Form 6765, Item A addresses the <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-280cc-rd-credit-deduction-disallowance\">IRC § 280C</a> reduced credit election (requiring an e-file statement named Form6765ItemASection280C.pdf when deductions or capitalized expenses are reduced, and specifying that while a controlled group member may make the § 280C election, only the common parent under Treas. Reg. § 1.1502-77(a)(1) may make the election on behalf of a consolidated group), while Item B (<em>Controlled Group/Common Control</em>) explicitly asks whether the taxpayer is a member of a controlled group of corporations or a business under common control under IRC § 41(f)(1). Taxpayers checking \"Yes\" on Item B must attach a mandatory group credit statement (named Form6765ItemBGroupCredit.pdf for e-filed returns) containing 14 specific data items for each member and the group: (1) Entity Employer Identification Number (EIN), (2) Entity Name, (3) Common Parent/Designated Member indicator, (4) Consolidated Member indicator, (5) Principal Business Activity (PBA) Code, (6) Wages QREs, (7) Supplies QREs, (8) Rent/Lease Computers QREs, (9) <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41b3-contract-research-expenses\">Contract Research QREs</a>, (10) Total QREs of the Controlled Member, (11) Percentage of credit allocated, (12) Portion of credit allocated to the member (reflecting the reduced credit if Item A is elected), (13) Total QREs of the controlled group on the final line, and (14) Total credit amount for the controlled group on the final line, accompanied by the number of controlled group members in the current and previous years and an indication of whether the QREs are claimed on one consolidated controlled group return or separate returns."
      }
