        "text": "Consider a high-technology startup engineering consulting firm that does not possess an Applicable Financial Statement (non-AFS taxpayer). Prior to the first day of the taxable year, the firm establishes accounting procedures to expense on its financial books and records all tangible property items costing under $2,000. Although the firm memorializes this $2,000 threshold in a written accounting policy manual as an <a href=\"/us/glossary/rd-tax-credit-audit-defense\">audit-defense</a> best practice, under 26 CFR § 1.263(a)-1(f)(1)(ii)(B) a non-AFS taxpayer's beginning-of-year accounting procedures are not legally required by regulation to be in writing. During the taxable year, the firm purchases a specialized 3D printer extruder accessory for $450 to fabricate an experimental prototype, records the $450 as a current expense on its books and records, and attaches the required statement titled <em>\"Section 1.263(a)-1(f) de minimis safe harbor election\"</em> to its timely filed original federal income tax return."
