        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "In Regulatory Example 14 (26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(viii)), Taxpayer X develops software used both for internal G&A functions that facilitate or support X's trade or business and to allow third parties to initiate functions and review data. Taxpayer X incurs $50,000 in total research expenditures for the software and successfully isolates Subset A, a pure Third-Party Subset accounting for 50% ($25,000) of total expenditures. The remaining Dual-Function Subset of the software, Subset B (accounting for the remaining 50%, or $25,000, of expenditures), allows third parties to review data while simultaneously providing X with data used in its internal G&A functions; X is unable to isolate a separate third-party subset within Subset B. At the beginning of software development, X applies an objective, reasonable industry method—processing time—and determines that third-party use of Subset B is reasonably anticipated to account for 15% of the total processing time of Subset B, satisfying the ≥10% usage threshold of 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(vi)(C). Assuming all underlying research activities satisfy IRC § 41(d) (without regard to § 41(d)(4)(E)) and the expenditures qualify under IRC § 41(b), X includes 100% of Subset A's allocable QREs ($25,000 × 100% = $25,000) pursuant to 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(vi)(B) plus 25% of Subset B's allocable QREs ($25,000 × 25% = $6,250) pursuant to the 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(vi)(C) safe harbor, yielding $31,250 in total credit-eligible QREs (62.5% of the $50,000 total software development budget). Similarly, in an application example analyzed in practitioner guidance under the final regulations, a bank develops a web application costing $100,000 with 100 total user interface screens: 75 screens allow customers to check balances, transfer funds, and deposit checks (isolated as a Third-Party Subset yielding $75,000 × 100% = $75,000 in QREs), while 25 screens comprise an interwoven data-mining and G&A subset where 3 of the 25 screens (12%, exceeding the 10% threshold) facilitate completing customer interactions, allowing 25% of the remaining $25,000 ($6,250) to qualify under the safe harbor for a total QRE recovery of $81,250."
