        "text": "This creates a pervasive <strong>\"Customization Trap\"</strong> for taxpayers implementing or heavily customizing G&A platforms such as ERP, <a href=\"/us/faq/crm-customization-rd-tax-credit-eligibility\">CRM</a>, human capital management, or financial accounting suites. Even when an enterprise spends millions of dollars customizing an acquired ERP package, attempting to qualify the G&A core under the <a href=\"/us/faq/internal-use-software-rd-tax-credit\">IUS rules</a> almost always fails because: (1) the underlying ERP package is commercially available in the marketplace under 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(vii)(A)(3); (2) custom extensions and interfaces rarely involve such severe technical capability or methodology risk that the company's capital investment might not be recovered within a reasonable period under 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(vii)(C); and (3) template selection, parameter configuration, and one-to-one data mapping are excluded from Section 41(d) under 26 CFR § 1.41-4(a)(8) and capitalized under IRC § 263(a) per PLR 200236028 and CCA 201549024. Consequently, whenever an enterprise software initiative combines internal G&A administration with external third-party interaction, the Dual Function Software framework of 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(vi) represents the primary viable legal pathway to recover research tax credits."
