        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "<strong>26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(viii) Example 6</strong> (<em>Internal use software; definition of third party</em>) directly illustrates the operation of this vendor exclusion. In Example 6, Taxpayer X develops software to interact electronically with its vendors to improve X's inventory management; X develops the software specifically to enable X to interact with vendors and to allow vendors to initiate functions (such as replenishment orders) or review data (such as inventory levels) on X's system. Because inventory management is explicitly listed as a G&A financial management function under 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(iii)(B)(1), the vendors are persons using the software to support the general and administrative functions of the taxpayer. Consequently, under 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(vi)(E), those vendors do not qualify as \"third parties,\" and the vendor-facing inventory software is classified in its entirety as pure <strong>Internal Use Software</strong> (subject to the three-part HTIT), not as Third-Party Interaction Software or Dual Function Software."
