      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Under 26 U.S.C. § 41(c)(5)(A) and Treasury Regulation 26 CFR § 1.41-3(d)(1), in computing the research credit for taxable years beginning after December 31, 1989, the QREs and gross receipts taken into account in computing a taxpayer's Fixed-Base Percentage and Base Amount must be determined on a basis consistent with the definition and determination of QREs and gross receipts for the credit year, without regard to the law in effect for the taxable years taken into account in computing the FBP or Base Amount. Furthermore, this consistency mandate applies regardless of whether the statutory period of limitations for filing a claim for credit or refund has expired for any base-period taxable year, and Chapter 6 of the IRS Audit Techniques Guide confirms that consistency requires symmetrical treatment both between credit-year QREs and base-year QREs and between base-year gross receipts and the preceding four-year AAGR."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Treasury Regulation 26 CFR § 1.41-3(d)(2) provides two concrete illustrations of how the <a href=\"/us/glossary/consistency-rule\">consistency rule</a> operates bilaterally. In 26 CFR § 1.41-3(d)(2) Example 1 (Downward Base Adjustment), calendar-year accrual Taxpayer X computes its research credit for 2001 after having reported $100x (1984), $120x (1985), $150x (1986), $180x (1987), and $170x (1988)—totaling $720x—as QREs on its 1984–1988 Forms 6765 based on the statutory definition of qualified research in effect for those years. Because Congress narrowed the statutory definition of qualified research for taxable years beginning after December 31, 1985, applying the credit-year definition retroactively to 1984 and 1985 reduces X's allowable QREs for those two years to $80x and $100x, respectively; under 26 U.S.C. § 41(c)(5) and 26 CFR § 1.41-3(d)(1), X must reduce its 1984–1988 aggregate base-period QREs from $720x to $680x ($80x + $100x + $150x + $180x + $170x = $680x) when computing its FBP. Conversely, in 26 CFR § 1.41-3(d)(2) Example 2 (Upward Base Adjustment), if Taxpayer X newly claims in the credit year that a particular category of expenditure qualifies as a QRE—reflecting a change in position because X had not previously treated similar expenditures as QREs—the consistency rule compels X to increase its 1984–1988 QREs in the numerator of its FBP to include any similar expenditures paid or incurred during the 1984–1988 fixed-base period, even though those base years are closed by the statute of limitations."
      },
      {
        "type": "subheading",
        "text": "IRS Audit Procedures, Case Law Precedent, and Initial IDR Substantiation (I.R.C. § 6001)"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Chapter 6 (<em>The Consistency Requirement</em>, updated July 2, 2026) of the IRS Audit Techniques Guide establishes four core diagnostic questions for auditing a taxpayer's FBP:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "ordered": true,
        "items": [
          "\"Is the fixed-base percentage (the ratio of 84-88 QREs to gross receipts) in the base years substantially lower than current research ratios? If so, why?\"",
          "\"Do past annual reports or 10Ks support the reported base years' QREs?\"",
          "\"If the research credit was claimed in prior years, were the same base years' attributes used? If not, why not?\"",
          "\"Was there a prior research credit examination? Did it cover one or more of the base years?\""
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Crucially, ATG Chapter 6 instructs examiners that taxpayers may not rely upon mathematical extrapolation of recent years' data as support for the Fixed-Base Percentage computation; actual base-year records must be analyzed. In support of this substantiation standard, ATG Chapter 6 (Footnote 14) cites <em>Research, Inc. v. United States</em>, 95-1 USTC ¶ 50,407 (D. Minn. 1995) (also reported at 1995 WL 560140). Although decided under the pre-1990 \"rolling base period\" rules—and, as tax legal commentary notes, representing a federal district court holding rather than an automatic invalidation rule for immaterial record gaps—<em>Research, Inc.</em> directly illustrates the evidentiary consequences of failing to substantiate a consistent baseline. There, the taxpayer included expenses for \"special system projects\" in its credit-year QREs and admitted incurring the same type of project expenses during the base period, but had destroyed the relevant base-period documentation and therefore could not quantify its base-period QREs; the District of Minnesota disallowed the credit because the relative increase in QREs could not be measured without accounting for base-period expenses on the same class of projects. Recent Tax Court jurisprudence continues to reinforce strict base-period substantiation; as Grant Thornton reported in February 2026 regarding <em>George v. Commissioner</em>, when a taxpayer failed to substantiate base-period QREs under the ASC, the Tax Court declined to apply the <em>Cohan</em> rule to estimate base-period QREs and instead restricted the taxpayer to the 6% statutory fallback rate."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Under I.R.C. § 6001, 26 CFR § 1.6001-1, and ATG Chapter 7 (<em>Substantiation and Recordkeeping</em>, updated July 2, 2026), taxpayers bear the affirmative burden of retaining records in sufficiently usable form and detail to substantiate every component of the FBP and Base Amount. At the opening audit meeting, ATG Chapter 7 directs examiners to issue \"bite-sized\" <a href=\"/us/glossary/information-document-request-idr\">Information Document Requests</a> (IDRs) with enforced turnaround times specifically requesting the taxpayer's base amount and fixed-base percentage calculations, general information including the chart of accounts and organization charts, and complete records of corporate acquisitions and dispositions from 1984 through the tax year under audit, supplemented where appropriate by annual reports, internal audit submissions, board minutes, project authorizations, and outside consultant studies. Courts permit estimation methods only where <a href=\"/us/glossary/contemporaneous-documentation\">contemporaneous records</a> do not exist, and then only if (1) the taxpayer first establishes that it engaged in qualified research activities as defined in IRC § 41(d), (2) the failure to maintain a proper system to capture relevant information is not an \"inexactitude of their own making,\" (3) the sole issue is the exact dollar amount paid or incurred in the qualified research activity, and (4) the taxpayer provides factual support for every assumption underlying its estimates. Where recordkeeping remains deficient, ATG Chapter 7 advises examiners to consider issuing a formal Notice of Inadequate Records under 26 CFR § 1.6001-1(d)."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Policy and Compliance Analysis: Recommendations"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "ordered": true,
        "items": [
          "<strong>Legislative Reform to Modernize the 1984–1988 Base Period:</strong> From a tax administration standpoint, eliminating the requirement to preserve and audit forty-year-old ledgers from 1984–1988 would reduce substantiation disputes under I.R.C. § 6001 while aligning the RRC benchmark with contemporary corporate operations.",
          "<strong>Internal Mandate for a Perpetual FBP Historical Compliance File and Utilization of IRS RCRAs:</strong> Complementing this internal governance protocol, Large Business and International (formerly Large and Mid-Size Business, LMSB) taxpayers should consider executing a formal Research Credit Recordkeeping Agreement (RCRA) with the IRS pursuant to Notice 2004-11 (published February 9, 2004), which—as highlighted in Chapter 7 of the IRS Audit Techniques Guide—enables the taxpayer and the IRS to agree upfront upon completion of an examination cycle on the exact prospective records required to substantiate future research credit claims."
        ]
      }
