      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Under <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41h-payroll-tax-credit-qualified-small-businesses\">IRC § 41(h)(2)</a>, for any taxable year, the \"payroll tax credit portion\" of the research credit determined under IRC § 41(a) is the least of: (A) the amount specified in the taxpayer's payroll tax election, (B) the research credit determined under IRC § 41(a) for the taxable year (before the application of IRC § 41(h)), or (C) in the case of a qualified small business other than a partnership or S corporation, the amount of the business credit carryforward under IRC § 39 carried from that taxable year (determined before the application of IRC § 41(h)). Under IRC § 41(h)(4)(A) and IRS administrative guidance (Notice 2017-23 and the Instructions for Form 6765, Form 3800, and Form 8974), the election must be made on Form 6765 (Line 36, or Line 44 for tax years beginning before January 1, 2024) attached to a timely filed original return (including extensions)—at the entity level for a partnership (Form 1065 under IRC § 6031) or S corporation (Form 1120-S under IRC § 6037) under IRC § 41(h)(4)(C)—cannot be made on an amended return, and may be revoked only with the consent of the Secretary. Under IRC § 41(h)(1) and the IRS Instructions for Form 3800, the elected payroll tax credit portion is not treated as an income tax credit under IRC § 41(a) (other than for purposes of the IRC § 280C deduction reduction), cannot offset current-year income tax liability, and must be subtracted from any <a href=\"/us/glossary/research-credit-carryforward\">research credit carryforward</a> reported on Form 3800, Part II, Line 34."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Once elected, the credit is claimed on <a href=\"/us/glossary/irs-form-8974\">Form 8974</a> attached to Form 941 (Employer's Quarterly Federal Tax Return, or annual Forms 943/944) starting in the first calendar quarter that begins after the date the QSB files its income tax return reflecting the election. On Form 8974, Part 2, Line 12 applies the credit against the employer's 50 percent share of Social Security tax up to $250,000 per quarter, Line 16 applies any remaining credit (Line 13) against the employer's 50 percent share of Medicare tax (Line 15), and Line 17 transfers the combined credit to Form 941, Line 11. Businesses utilizing a Section 3504 agent, a Certified Professional Employer Organization (CPEO), or a non-certified PEO claim the credit on the aggregate Form 941 filed under the agent's, CPEO's, or PEO's EIN, supported by Schedule R (Form 941) (Allocation Schedule for Aggregate Form 941 Filers) and a separate Form 8974 for each electing QSB client."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Specifically, under IRC § 41(h)(3)(A)(i)–(ii), a corporation, partnership, or other person (excluding tax-exempt organizations under IRC § 501 per § 41(h)(3)(B)) qualifies as a \"<a href=\"/us/glossary/qualified-small-business-qsb\">qualified small business</a>\" for the payroll tax offset only if: (I) its gross receipts (as determined under the computation rules of IRC § 448(c)(3), <em>without regard to subparagraph (A) thereof</em>) for the taxable year are <strong>less than $5,000,000</strong>, and (II) it did not have gross receipts (as so determined) for any taxable year preceding the 5-taxable-year period ending with such taxable year. In addition, under IRC § 41(h)(4)(B)(ii), a taxpayer may not make the <a href=\"/us/glossary/payroll-tax-credit-election\">payroll tax election</a> if the taxpayer (or any person aggregated as a single taxpayer under IRC § 41(h)(5)(A) and § 41(f)(1)) has made an election under IRC § 41(h) for 5 or more preceding taxable years. Under IRC § 41(h)(5)(A)–(B), all members of a controlled group or businesses under <a href=\"/us/glossary/common-control\">common control</a> are aggregated as a single taxpayer for the $5,000,000 gross receipts test and the 5-year test, and the $500,000 annual election cap (each of the two $250,000 amounts in § 41(h)(4)(B)(i)) is allocated proportionately among the members in the same manner as under IRC § 41(f)(1). Because IRC § 41(h)(3)(A)(i)(I) cross-references only the computational mechanics of IRC § 448(c)(3) (such as short-year annualization and reduction for returns and allowances) while imposing its own hard statutory ceiling of less than $5,000,000 for the current taxable year, startups with current-year gross receipts of $5,000,000 or more are statutorily ineligible for the IRC § 41(h) payroll tax offset."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Strategic Conclusions and Analytical Recommendations"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Because IRC § 38(c) caps annual utilization at Net Income Tax less the 25 percent floor above $25,000 (or TMT for non-specified credits of non-corporate taxpayers), high-R&D enterprises routinely accumulate substantial credit carryforwards that require multi-decade tax planning."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "First, tax departments should construct multi-scenario sensitivity models of the IRC § 38(c) ceiling across IRC §§ 174 and 174A regimes to quantify how shifts in the taxable income base alter credit absorption. Specifically, following the enactment of Public Law 119–21 (signed July 4, 2025) and IRC § 174A, taxpayers transitioning out of mandatory 5-year domestic R&E amortization for tax years beginning after December 31, 2024—or electing under Section 70302(f)(2) of Public Law 119–21 and Rev. Proc. 2025-28 to deduct unamortized 2022–2024 domestic R&E balances in full in 2025 or ratably across 2025 and 2026—must model the resulting compression of their Net Income Tax (NIT) and the corresponding reduction in current-year GBC absorption capacity. Where immediate domestic expensing under IRC § 174A(a) would eliminate current-year tax liability and trap expiring GBC carryforwards, planners should evaluate whether electing 60-month amortization under IRC § 174A(c) or 10-year amortization under IRC § 59(e) preserves sufficient NIT to absorb older credit vintages."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Crucially, this tracker must record whether the taxpayer elected the 21 percent reduced research credit under <a href=\"/us/glossary/section-280c-election\">IRC § 280C(c)(2)</a> (formerly IRC § 280C(c)(3)) in each origination year, as only unreduced IRC § 41(a) credits qualify for the mandatory Year 21 expiration deduction (or earlier cessation deduction) under IRC § 196(a)–(c)(4)."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Third, tax advisors should implement integrated 5-year startup liquidity and 20-year corporate utilization forecasting. For early-stage enterprises that satisfy the strict IRC § 41(h)(3)(A) QSB eligibility criteria (under $5,000,000 in current-year gross receipts and no gross receipts prior to the 5-taxable-year period ending with the credit year), tax planners must align Form 6765 elections on timely filed original returns with quarterly Form 8974 and Form 941 <a href=\"/us/faq/how-to-apply-rd-tax-credit-to-payroll-taxes\">payroll tax liabilities</a> to capture up to $500,000 annually ($250,000 per quarter against employer Social Security tax on Form 8974, Line 12, plus remaining excess against employer Medicare tax on Line 16). For established corporations and controlled groups subject to the single apportioned $25,000 threshold under IRC § 38(c)(6)(B), 5-year and 20-year capital budgeting forecasts must pair projected QRE growth under IRC § 41(f)(1) with projected Net Income Tax and Corporate Alternative Minimum Tax (CAMT) trajectories to prevent credit expiration at the end of the IRC § 39 carryforward horizon."
      }
