        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Under federal tax law, the taxpayer bears the affirmative burden of proving entitlement to all claimed QREs and demonstrating that no claimed expenditures derive from activities excluded under IRC § 41(d)(4). Heightened IRS examination scrutiny, formal disclosure requirements on updated tax forms, and recent judicial decisions denying R&D credits to engineering and design firms for inadequate substantiation underscore that merely presenting the eventual technical solution is legally insufficient. Taxpayers must maintain <a href=\"/us/glossary/contemporaneous-documentation\">contemporaneous project documentation</a> establishing a direct technical nexus between specific employee wage hours, supply costs, and contractor invoices and the iterative resolution of hard-science technical uncertainties, while meticulously tracking and excluding employee time spent on market research, efficiency surveys, cosmetic design, or psychological studies."
