        "text": "In <strong>26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(viii) Example 17</strong> (<em>Internal use software; application of the high threshold of innovation test</em>), taxpayer X seeks to expand internal computing power for G&A computations without purchasing new hardware by developing a screen-saver-like application running on thousands of idle employee PCs and workstations that fetches and executes jobs from a central server until the job finishes or the employee resumes working on the computer. Because the software supports G&A computations and is not intended for commercial sale or third-party interaction, it is IUS. However, it satisfies the HTI test because: (1) avoiding new hardware purchases provides a substantial, economically significant cost reduction or speed improvement (Innovative); (2) at the outset, no commercial application existed with this capability (Not Commercially Available); and (3) X committed substantial resources while facing substantial technical uncertainty regarding the capability of developing a server application able to schedule and distribute jobs across thousands of PCs and workstations and handle all user machine error conditions within a reasonable recovery period (Significant Economic Risk)."
