          "<strong>Documenting Substantial Technical Uncertainty and Refund Specificity (26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(vii)(C) and CCM 20214101F) (Phase II):</strong> For pure IUS projects (and DFS subsets not using the 25% safe harbor), <a href=\"/us/faq/documenting-process-of-experimentation-rd-credit\">record the process of experimentation</a> (§ 1.41-4(a)(5)) and establish that substantial uncertainty regarding capability or methodology (not merely routine appropriate design choices, which Section VII.A of the T.D. 9786 Preamble and Example 18 note rarely qualify) existed at the beginning of activities. Additionally, when claiming a research credit refund on an amended return (where Form 6765 Section G Column 49(f) is required, and the IRS 45-day perfection period has been extended through January 10, 2027), satisfy <a href=\"/us/faq/irs-ccm-20214101f-impact-rd-claims\">IRS Chief Counsel Memorandum 20214101F</a> (issued Sept. 17, 2021; released Oct. 15, 2021) and 26 CFR § 301.6402-2(b)(1) within the 26 U.S.C. § 6511 limitations period (3 years from return filing or 2 years from tax payment) by specifying under penalties of perjury: (i) all business components, (ii) all research activities performed, (iii) all individuals performing each activity (or breaking out wages across actual conduct in Column 50, direct supervision in Column 51, and direct support in Column 52), (iv) the information each individual sought to discover (Column 49(f)), and (v) total qualified wage, supply, and contract expenses, with exact page citations to any attached study.",
          "<strong>Quantifying Substantial and Economically Significant Improvements (26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(vii)(B) & (D)) (Phase II):</strong> Establish pre-development quantitative baselines (cost, processing speed, throughput) and project the objective, measurable, substantial, and economically significant improvement attributable solely to the new or improved software, independent of any modifications to related hardware or other software (§ 1.41-4(c)(6)(vii)(D)).",
          "<strong>Substantiating Commercial Unavailability and Vendor Contract Rights/Risk (26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(vii)(A)(3), § 1.41-2(e), and § 1.41-4A(d)) (Phase II):</strong> Document market gap analyses proving that no off-the-shelf software could be purchased, leased, or licensed and used for the intended purpose without modifications meeting the Innovative and Significant Economic Risk requirements (noting that vendor product evaluation itself is excluded from experimentation under 26 CFR § 1.41-4(a)(8) Example 5), and ensure all third-party contractor agreements (26 CFR § 1.41-2(e)) are executed prior to research performance, grant the taxpayer rights to the research results, place non-contingent financial risk on the taxpayer, and preserve the taxpayer's substantial rights under the <a href=\"/us/glossary/funded-research\">funded research</a> rules of 26 CFR § 1.41-4A(d).",
          "<strong>Applying IRS Software Audit Guidelines Accurately (I.R.C. § 41(d)(1)(C) and § 41(d)(4)) (Phase III):</strong> Use the <em>IRS Audit Guidelines on the Application of the Process of Experimentation for All Software</em> for its actual administrative scope—screening out software activities rated by the IRS as \"High Risk\" of failing I.R.C. § 41(d)(1)(C) across all software (specifically Maintenance of Existing Software Applications/Products, such as minor releases 1.1/1.2, corrective debugging, security patches, and perfective enhancements; Software Application Configuration of vendor-purchased packages; rewriting an application in a new language; writing hardware device drivers from vendor specifications; data quality/cleansing/consistency formatting; and bundling existing individual software products into suites, as well as post-production debugging under § 41(d)(4)(A) and <a href=\"/us/glossary/adaptation-exclusion\">customer adaptation</a> under § 41(d)(4)(B))—while recognizing that the Audit Guidelines explicitly do not address the IUS exclusion under § 41(d)(4)(E).",
          "<strong>Reconciling GAAP (ASC 350-40 / ASU 2025-06) and Tax (IRC § 174A / § 174 and IRC § 41) Cost Pools (Phase III):</strong> Maintain a formal accounting bridge reconciling software costs capitalized or expensed under U.S. GAAP (ASC 350-40, including costs expensed prior to resolving \"significant development uncertainty\" under ASU 2025-06 ASC 350-40-25-12A or expensed under ASC 730 via ASC 350-40-15-7) with domestic R&E expenditures deducted under 26 U.S.C. § 174A (including any 2025/2026 transition deductions for unamortized 2022–2024 domestic R&E under OBBBA), foreign R&E amortized over 15 years under § 174 (excluded from § 41 under § 41(d)(4)(F)), and domestic QREs claimed on Form 6765 (including the <a href=\"/us/glossary/section-280c-election\">26 U.S.C. § 280C(c) election</a> on Item A).",
          "<strong>Annual Multi-Year Project Review and Recalibration (Phase IV):</strong> Audit ongoing multi-year software projects annually to verify whether functional pivots—such as adding G&A modules to a commercial product or adding third-party portals to an internal G&A system—have created a separate improvement business component under 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(v) or altered anticipated third-party usage relative to the 10% DFS safe harbor threshold.",
          "<strong>Pre-Development Specialist Consultation (Phase IV):</strong> Engage <a href=\"/us/faq/cpa-vs-specialist-rd-tax-credit\">R&D tax credit specialists</a> alongside software architects at project initiation so that inception-date intent (§ 1.41-4(c)(6)(v)), third-party subset boundaries (§ 1.41-4(c)(6)(vi)(B)), and HTI technical baselines (§ 1.41-4(c)(6)(vii)) are captured contemporaneously during engineering sprints rather than reconstructed retroactively during an IRS examination."
