      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Strategic Compliance Governance and Actionable Protocols"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Integrating the statutory mandates of 26 U.S.C. § 41(b) and the regulatory directives of 26 CFR § 1.41-2(b) and § 1.174-2 with IRS examination standards yields five concrete governance protocols for corporate tax departments, engineering leaders, and accounting teams."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "First, corporate tax and accounting departments should establish a codified R&D supplies classification matrix across procurement and financial accounting. Organizations must implement a formal internal accounting policy mapping raw material inventory items, laboratory consumables, custom tooling, and pilot model components against the statutory gates of 26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(2)(C) and 26 CFR § 1.41-2(b)(1). This internal policy must proactively segregate depreciable capital equipment governed by IRC § 167, leased personal property disallowed under 26 CFR § 1.41-2(b)(4), software licenses, patent attorney fees, and indirect overhead from R&D supply general ledger accounts prior to tax return preparation."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Second, enterprises must align ERP project coding directly with Form 6765 Section G business components. Because Section G of redesigned Form 6765 requires reporting \"Cost of supplies\" (Column 54) by unique business component alphanumeric identifier (Column 49(c)) for tax years beginning after 2025 and on all amended returns claiming or increasing a research credit, taxpayers can no longer rely on lump-sum year-end estimates extracted from generic general ledger \"Materials\" or \"Cost of Goods Sold\" accounts. Purchase orders and ERP inventory requisitions must enforce mandatory Business Component and Project Code tagging at the exact point of material check-out."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Third, physical manufacturers should institute a formal pilot model and custom tooling accounting review protocol. For manufacturers fabricating pilot models under 26 CFR § 1.174-2(a)(4) or customer-ordered production tooling governed by <em>T.G. Missouri Corp. v. Commissioner</em>, 133 T.C. 278 (2009), tax and fixed-asset accounting teams must review customer contracts and capital asset ledgers concurrently. Teams must verify that only design, fabrication, and testing costs incurred prior to the elimination of technical uncertainty are captured in accordance with 26 CFR § 1.174-2(a)(2), avoiding any reliance on the misconception that the 80% activity rule under 26 CFR § 1.41-4(a)(6) sweeps in post-uncertainty commercial production costs. Concurrently, accounting personnel must verify whether the resulting pilot model or mold is scrapped, sold to the customer without taxpayer depreciation, or retained as internal depreciable tooling under IRC § 167."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Fourth, facilities and tax engineering teams must deploy sub-metering or engineering baseline studies before claiming extraordinary utilities. Because Chapter 10.b.2 of the IRS ATG: <em>Research Credit Issues</em> explicitly rejects square-footage electricity comparisons between administrative and R&D buildings, taxpayers operating high-energy research equipment should install dedicated utility sub-meters or commission contemporaneous engineering load studies to isolate extraordinary utility consumption under 26 CFR § 1.41-2(b)(2)(ii)."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Fifth, corporate tax departments should prepare contemporaneous tax position memoranda for high-scrutiny supply categories. Whenever claimed supply QREs constitute a substantial percentage of total QREs—triggering the <a href=\"/us/faq/what-triggers-irs-rd-tax-credit-audit\">examination alert</a> in Section 4.b of the IRS ATG: <em>Qualified Research Expenses</em>—or involve mixed-use inventory allocations or customer-sold pilot models, tax counsel and internal auditors should assemble a pre-filing Tax Position Memorandum linking the transactional vendor invoices, general ledger entries, engineering bills of materials, customer contracts, and controlling legal authorities."
      }
