        "text": "At the same time, Treasury Regulation § 1.41-4(c)(2)(iii) and (iv) establish critical carve-outs that prevent examiners from overextending the RACP exclusion. Under § 1.41-4(c)(2)(iii), when a taxpayer develops both a product and a manufacturing or commercial production process for that product, the RACP exclusion applies separately to the product and the process. Similarly, under § 1.41-4(c)(2)(iv), <a href=\"/us/glossary/clinical-trials-rd-credit\">clinical testing of a pharmaceutical product</a> in the United States prior to domestic commercial production is not treated as RACP even if the drug is already commercially available in other countries, and additional clinical testing conducted after a drug has received Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approval for a specific therapeutic use is not treated as occurring after the beginning of commercial production if undertaken to establish new functional uses, characteristics, indications, combinations, dosages, or delivery forms that must be approved by the FDA."
