        "text": "These Section 41 exclusions operate alongside parallel disallowances under <a href=\"/us/faq/section-41-credit-vs-section-174-expenses\">IRC § 174</a> and related Section 41 regulatory provisions. Specifically, in current Treasury Regulations, 26 CFR § 1.174-2(a)(6)(i)–(vii) (which appeared as § 1.174-2(a)(3) in earlier regulatory editions and is cited as § 1.174-2(a)(3) in the June 2005 IRS <em>Qualified Research Activities</em> Audit Techniques Guide) excludes from <a href=\"/us/glossary/research-and-experimental-expenditures\">research and experimental expenditures</a>: (i) the ordinary testing or inspection of materials or products for quality control (further defined in § 1.174-2(a)(7) as testing or inspection to determine whether particular units of materials or products conform to specified parameters, though quality control testing does not include testing to determine if the design of the product is appropriate); (ii) efficiency surveys; (iii) management studies; (iv) consumer surveys; (v) advertising or promotions; (vi) the acquisition of another's patent, model, production, or process; and (vii) research in connection with literary, historical, or similar projects. Likewise, IRC § 41(d)(4)(G) and 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(8) exclude research in the <a href=\"/us/glossary/social-sciences-exclusion\">social sciences</a> (including economics, business management, and behavioral sciences), arts, or humanities. Because IRC § 41(d)(4)(D)(iv) and 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(5)(iv) establish an express categorical bar, any employee wages, supplies, or third-party contractor costs attributable to routine data collection are legally <a href=\"/us/faq/excluded-expenses-rd-tax-credit\">ineligible to be claimed as QREs</a>."
