        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Once Business Component A qualifies under Scenario 2, consider the individual employee wage evaluation under IRC § 41(b)(2)(B) and 26 CFR § 1.41-2(d)(2) for a hypothetical Lead Materials Engineer earning <strong>$150,000</strong> in IRC § 3401(a) Form W-2 taxable wages across <strong>2,000 annual working hours</strong> (excluding sick leave and vacation per the IRS <em>Qualified Research Expenses</em> ATG). In <strong>Case A (Employee Meets the ≥80% Threshold)</strong>, the engineer spends <strong>1,640 hours</strong> conducting and directly supervising qualified composite vessel experimentation on Business Component A, and <strong>360 hours</strong> performing excluded routine factory autoclave data collection (1,640 + 360 = 2,000 hours). Because the engineer's qualified-services fraction under 26 CFR § 1.41-2(d)(2) is 1,640 ÷ 2,000 = 0.8200 (<strong>82.0% ≥ 80.0%</strong>), \"substantially all\" of the engineer's services for the taxable year consist of qualified services, allowing <strong>100%</strong> of the engineer's taxable wages ($150,000 × 100% = $150,000) to be claimed as in-house wage QREs. In <strong>Case B (Employee Falls Below the 80% Threshold)</strong>, the engineer spends <strong>1,450 hours</strong> on qualified composite vessel experimentation and <strong>550 hours</strong> on excluded routine data collection (1,450 + 550 = 2,000 hours). Because the qualified-services fraction is 1,450 ÷ 2,000 = 0.7250 (<strong>72.5% &lt; 80.0%</strong>), the 100% round-up of 26 CFR § 1.41-2(d)(2) is unavailable, and only the actual pro-rata fraction of qualified services may be claimed: $150,000 × 0.725 = $108,750 in eligible Form W-2 wage QREs, while $150,000 × 0.275 = $41,250 (27.5% attributable to excluded RDC) must be excluded from QREs."
