        "text": "Under Scenario A (Time-and-Materials Hourly Billing), DevShop contracts to perform AI research and development billed monthly at $200 per hour regardless of whether the AI works; after expending 1,000 hours, even if the research fails completely, DevShop collects $200,000 (1,000 hours × $200/hour = $200,000) and suffers $0 in unrecovered financial exposure to failure, rendering DevShop's work 100% funded and ineligible under 26 CFR Section 1.41-4A(d)(1). Provided the agreement was executed prior to performance, the research was performed on the client's behalf (meaning the client has a right to the research results under 26 CFR Section 1.41-2(e)(3)), and the underlying work satisfies IRC Section 41(d)(1), the client may evaluate treating 65% of the non-contingent payment—$130,000 (0.65 × $200,000 = $130,000)—as a <a href=\"/us/faq/outsourced-contractor-rd-tax-credit-eligibility\">qualified contract research expense</a> under IRC Section 41(b)(3)(A) and 26 CFR Section 1.41-2(e)(1)–(2). Under Scenario B (Fixed Bid Contingent on Technical User Acceptance Testing [UAT]), DevShop agrees to a $200,000 flat fee payable solely upon passing technical UAT while retaining non-exclusive substantial rights. If resolving technical uncertainties consumes 2,000 engineering hours (representing $200,000 in internal QREs at an illustrative internal cost of $100/hour, or $400,000 at a $200/hour billing equivalent) and the software fails UAT, DevShop receives $0 and absorbs 100% of its internal costs. Under <em>Fairchild</em>, <em>Geosyntec</em>, <em>Meyer, Borgman & Johnson</em>, and <em>Smith</em>, however, the UAT criteria in the contract must test concrete, measurable technical specifications resolving technological uncertainty rather than general commercial satisfaction or routine professional standards of care."
