        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Together, Examples 15 through 18 of Treas. Reg. § 1.41-4(c)(6)(viii) establish a critical compliance paradox: <strong>engineering competence and project confidence can be fatal to an IUS tax credit claim</strong> if project documentation frames technical hurdles as manageable implementation or design tasks rather than existential capability or methodological unknowns. If internal project charters or capital appropriation requests state that delivery within the budgeted schedule is highly certain—or if the technical challenge is limited to migrating legacy mainframe code as in Example 16 or designing an ERP integration interface as in Example 18—IRS examiners will <a href=\"/us/faq/what-happens-if-rd-tax-credit-is-denied\">disallow the entire IUS claim</a> on the grounds that the activity constituted routine software engineering rather than qualified research."
