        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "In Texas, while tax summaries occasionally group Texas alongside California as a state that permits an immediate corporate income tax deduction under pre-TCJA Section 174, primary statutory verification against Texas Tax Code Chapter 171, Texas Comptroller STAR Memo 202512012M, and 34 Texas Administrative Code Section 3.599 clarifies the precise legal architecture. Texas does not impose a traditional corporate net income tax; rather, it levies a Franchise Tax based on a taxable entity's \"taxable margin,\" which is computed as Total Revenue minus the greatest of four statutory deductions: Cost of Goods Sold (COGS), compensation, 30% of Total Revenue, or $1,000,000. Wherever the Texas Franchise Tax statute cross-references the federal Internal Revenue Code—such as for computing eligible Cost of Goods Sold under Texas Tax Code Section 171.1012—Texas is statutorily tied to the 2007 Internal Revenue Code (as confirmed in Comptroller STAR Memo 202512012M). Furthermore, for purposes of the <a href=\"/us/faq/state-vs-federal-rd-tax-credits\">Texas Franchise Tax Research and Development Activities Credit</a> under 34 Texas Administrative Code Section 3.599 (which remains in effect through December 31, 2026), Texas conforms specifically to the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 in effect on December 31, 2011, including pre-TCJA IRC Section 174 and 26 CFR Section 1.174-2. Thus, Texas does not conform to TCJA Section 174 capitalization, but the compliance divergence manifests in Franchise Tax COGS computations and Texas R&D credit eligibility rather than a conventional state net income tax deduction."
