        "text": "First, the Tax Court rejected the <strong>Physical-Element / Novelty Proportion Method</strong>. Relying on the district court decision in <em>Trinity Industries, Inc. v. United States</em>, 691 F. Supp. 2d 688 (N.D. Tex. 2010), the taxpayer in <em>Little Sandy Coal</em> argued that its tanker barge project satisfied the 80% Substantially All Rule because the redesigned hull alone made up 90% of the vessel and over 80% of the vessel's physical systems differed from prior vessels. The Tax Court explicitly declined to follow <em>Trinity Industries</em>, holding that 26 C.F.R. § 1.41-4(a)(6) requires the 80% test to be applied with respect to <strong>research activities</strong>, not the physical elements or components of the product being developed. As Judge Halpern explained, the physical proportion of novel elements is not a reasonable basis for measuring research activities because the extent of experimentation does not vary in proportion to physical component size—a small, complex subsystem may require extensive experimentation, whereas a massive structural hull may involve little or no experimentation once designed."
