        "text": "Under <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41b3-contract-research-expenses\">26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(3)(A)</a> and 26 CFR § 1.41-2(e)(1), \"contract research expenses\" are generally limited to 65 percent of any amount paid or incurred by the taxpayer in carrying on a trade or business to any person (other than an employee of the taxpayer) for the performance on behalf of the taxpayer of (i) qualified research as defined in § 1.41-4 or § 1.41-4A, or (ii) services which, if performed by employees of the taxpayer, would constitute qualified services within the meaning of IRC § 41(b)(2)(B). Where a contract calls for both qualified research/services and non-qualified services, only <a href=\"/us/faq/contractor-expenses-65-percent-rule-rd-credit\">65 percent</a> of the portion attributable to qualified research or qualified services is eligible as a contract research expense. By statute, the 65 percent inclusion rate increases to 75 percent for amounts paid or incurred to a qualified research consortium (a tax-exempt IRC § 501(c)(3) or § 501(c)(6) organization organized and operated primarily to conduct scientific research that is not a private foundation, conducting research on behalf of the taxpayer and one or more unrelated taxpayers under IRC § 41(b)(3)(C)), and to 100 percent for amounts paid to an eligible small business (500 or fewer employees), institution of higher education (as defined in IRC § 3304(f)), or federal laboratory for qualified energy research under IRC § 41(b)(3)(D). Under IRC § 41(b)(3)(B) and 26 CFR § 1.41-2(e)(4), if any contract research expense paid or incurred during a taxable year is attributable to qualified research to be conducted after the close of that taxable year (prepaid amounts), the expense is treated as paid or incurred only during the period in which the qualified research is actually conducted (as illustrated in 26 CFR § 1.41-2(e)(5) Example 2)."
