        "text": "In <strong>Scenario C — TPS Segregation Fails (or Cannot Be Identified), But Total DFS Anticipates ≥10% Third-Party Use (26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(viii) Example 12)</strong>, Logistics Corp is unable to isolate Subset A (or an IRS examiner disallows the functional segregation because the B2B portal shares unsegregated code modules with G&A accounting), leaving $100,000 of unsegregated Dual-Function Software, <strong>but</strong> Logistics Corp documented at the beginning of development via an objective, reasonable method that third-party use of the overall DFS is anticipated to be 15% (≥10%). Here, Third-Party Subset QREs equal <strong>$0</strong>, but under 26 CFR § 1.41-4(c)(6)(vi)(C) and Example 12, the 25% Safe Harbor applies to the entire $100,000 of unsegregated DFS: 25% × $100,000 = <strong>$25,000</strong>. Thus, Total Eligible QREs equal <strong>$25,000</strong> (25% of total project QREs, with $75,000 disallowed—representing a $30,000 QRE reduction compared to Scenario A, but avoiding a complete $0 disallowance)."
