        "text": "Executive branch guidance from the California FTB directly shapes the calculation of the regular incremental credit base and the resulting R&TC Section 24440 disallowance. A critical component of the California regular credit computation on Section A of Form FTB 3523 (Line 11) is the taxpayer's California gross receipts denominator for the four preceding taxable years, which dictates the <a href=\"/us/faq/rd-tax-credit-base-amount-calculation\">base amount</a> (Line 12) that current-year QREs must exceed. In <strong>FTB Technical Advice Memorandum (TAM) 2022-01</strong> (and revised <strong>FTB Publication 1050</strong>), the agency adopted the Multistate Tax Commission's (MTC) August 2021 narrow interpretation of Public Law 86-272 (15 U.S.C. §§ 381–384) regarding internet sales and website activities. Under TAM 2022-01 and Publication 1050, if a company engages in non-protected interactive internet activities—such as offering online applications for non-sales job positions, placing internet cookies on customer computers to gather market research, or providing interactive post-sale assistance via electronic chat or email on a website—in a destination state where it lacks physical presence, the FTB deems the company to have exceeded P.L. 86-272 immunity and established income tax nexus in that destination state."
