        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Federal and state courts uniformly hold that tax credits are a matter of legislative grace, meaning statutes granting credits under 26 U.S.C. § 41 are strictly construed against the taxpayer, who bears the burden of proving strict entitlement through <a href=\"/us/glossary/contemporaneous-documentation\">contemporaneous documentation</a>. Two landmark decisions in the <a href=\"/us/faq/apparel-fashion-industry-rd-tax-credits\">apparel design sector</a>—one before the California Office of Tax Appeals and one before the United States Tax Court—illustrate how tribunals enforce the Section 174 uncertainty test (§ 41(d)(1)(A)), the technological in nature test (§ 41(d)(1)(B)(i)), the process of experimentation test (§ 41(d)(1)(C)), and the style, taste, cosmetic, or seasonal design exclusion (§ 41(d)(3)(B))."
