        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "In a second hypothetical scenario, an aerospace engineering contractor operating in Warner Robins, Georgia, designs high-energy Electronic Warfare (EW) jamming pods for the U.S. Department of Defense. Resolving severe thermal dissipation uncertainties requires iterative structural and aerodynamic modeling alongside destructive wind-tunnel testing of composite prototype housings. Because the contractor performs this work under a fixed-price contract that conditions payment on the pods passing rigorous military acceptance testing (placing the financial risk of failed research on the contractor under <em>Fairchild Industries</em>, 71 F.3d 868) and permits the contractor to use the underlying thermal management know-how in future commercial avionics projects without paying a license fee to the government (retaining substantial rights under <em>Lockheed Martin</em>, 210 F.3d 1366, and <em>Populous Holdings</em>, Docket No. 405-17)—and avoids the disqualifying DD Form 254 intelligence prohibitions or university IP assignments that defeated the claim in <em>Dynetics</em>, 121 Fed. Cl. 492, as well as the absence of rejection clauses that defeated the claim in <em>Meyer, Borgman & Johnson</em>, 100 F.4th 986—the contractor's research is unfunded under IRC § 41(d)(4)(H) and 26 CFR § 1.41-4A(d). Consequently, the W-2 wages of its Georgia-based aerospace engineers and the non-depreciable composite materials used to build the wind-tunnel test models qualify as in-house QREs under IRC § 41(b)(2) and O.C.G.A. § 48-7-40.12(a)(4), and excess state credits can be monetized against payroll withholding via Form IT-WH."
