          "<strong>Living Subjects and Feed as Qualifying \"Pilot Model\" Supply QREs:</strong> Rejecting the IRS's argument that raising experimental flocks of broiler chickens constituted ordinary commercial production and that prior vendor lab testing eliminated uncertainty, the Court applied the 2014 pilot-model regulations (26 CFR § 1.174-2(a)(4)) and held that because real-world farm variables (fluctuating temperatures, environmental conditions, and biological interactions) created taxpayer-specific uncertainty regarding performance under GOMI's operating conditions, physical test flocks raised to evaluate and resolve uncertainty regarding an improved product (healthier, more uniform broilers) function as <strong>pilot models</strong>, and the feed costs incurred to raise them qualify as supply QREs under IRC § 41(b)(2)(A)(ii). Furthermore, the Court held that GOMI's decision not to claim any employee wage QREs (because management concluded calculating wage allocations was not worth the effort) did not preclude GOMI from claiming supply QREs.",
          "<strong>Contemporaneous Production Logs Outweigh Retroactive Study Narratives:</strong> Across all evaluated projects, GOMI substantiated $14,315,418 in total QREs (22.7% of the amount originally claimed, with 77.3% disallowed), including $7,280,578 for 2013 probiotic gut-health trials (<em>Sporulin</em> and <em>Calsporin</em>, where GOMI hypothesized efficacy, tested, analyzed data, and iterated upon failure), $1,521,039 for a 2014 laryngotracheitis (LT) vector-vaccine priming trial, $398,520 for a 2014 <em>Ross 708</em> vs. <em>Cobb 500</em> genetic line trial (which the Court praised as the \"cleanest example of the scientific method\" due to its control group and slaughter-margin data analysis), and its 2012 <em>HatchPak</em> and <em>Tylan</em> trial. However, the Court allowed $0 for trials where GOMI's contemporaneous daily feed recipes and barn logs contradicted the retroactive study—including <em>Salinomycin</em> and <em>Phytase</em> enzyme trials (~$1.2 million in QREs at risk, where feed logs showed a constant 0.4 lbs/ton dosage rather than experimental variations), <em>Floramax</em> probiotics (~$680,000 in QREs at risk, where no records tracked which flocks received the additive), 2013 <em>HatchPak/Tylan</em> (~$2.87 million credit year partially disallowed because scientific uncertainty had already been resolved in 2012), and 2012–2013 LT vaccine delivery and <em>Vaxxitek</em> IBD trials (where witnesses could not establish whether trials occurred in 2012 or 2013 or set aside control flocks).",
