        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Under 26 C.F.R. § 1.174-2(a)(1), qualification depends on the nature of the activity to which the expenditures relate rather than the nature of the product or the level of technological advancement it represents; the ultimate success, failure, sale, or use of the product or pilot model is irrelevant to its eligibility under IRC § 174 (and § 174A), and costs may qualify even if paid or incurred after physical production begins so long as they are paid or incurred before uncertainty concerning the development or improvement of the product is eliminated. However, two strict cutoffs terminate supply QRE eligibility. First, under 26 C.F.R. § 1.174-2(a)(2), except where the shrinking-back or component rule of 26 C.F.R. § 1.174-2(a)(5) applies to isolate a specific uncertain subcomponent, costs paid or incurred in the production of a product after the elimination of uncertainty concerning its development or improvement are ineligible commercial production costs (as illustrated in the regulatory examples, where $10,000 of materials and labor to build a pilot model of a custom machine plus $1,000 of testing to resolve design uncertainty qualify under § 174, whereas $20,000 subsequently spent to fabricate the machine for sale to the customer after design uncertainty is resolved does not qualify). Second, under 26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(4)(A), any <a href=\"/us/glossary/research-after-commercial-production\">research conducted after the beginning of commercial production</a> of the business component—once the component meets basic functional and economic design specifications and passes validation testing—is statutorily excluded from \"qualified research\"."
