        "text": "To qualify as a contract research expense under 26 U.S.C. § 41(b)(3), an expenditure must satisfy the strict operational rules of 26 CFR § 1.41-2(e). Under Treasury Regulation § 1.41-2(e)(1), eligible payments encompass amounts paid or incurred in carrying on a trade or business to any person other than an employee for the performance on behalf of the taxpayer of either (i) qualified research as defined in § 1.41-4 or § 1.41-4A, or (ii) services which, if performed by employees of the taxpayer, would constitute \"<a href=\"/us/glossary/qualified-services\">qualified services</a>\" within the meaning of IRC § 41(b)(2)(B) (engaging in, directly supervising, or directly supporting qualified research). Where a third-party contract calls for both qualified research/services and non-qualified services, only 65 percent of the specific portion attributable to qualified research or qualified services is eligible. Furthermore, as the IRS <em><a href=\"/us/glossary/irs-audit-techniques-guide-atg\">Audit Techniques Guide</a>: Credit for Increasing Research Activities (i.e. Research Tax Credit) IRC § 41 - Qualified Research Expenses</em> (updated July 2, 2026) explains, examiners distinguish between a general \"service contract\" (where a vendor is paid by the hour and the research is not specified, requiring examiners to verify the actual qualified work performed) and a \"research contract\" (where there is an agreed amount to perform specified qualified research)."
