          "<strong>Technological in Nature Test (26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(1)(B)(i)):</strong> The research must be undertaken for the purpose of discovering information that is technological in nature, meaning the process of experimentation fundamentally relies on principles of the physical or biological sciences, engineering, or computer science.",
          "<strong>Business Component / Permitted Purpose Test (26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(1)(B)(ii) & § 41(d)(2)–(3)):</strong> The application of the discovered information must be intended to be useful in the development of a new or improved business component of the taxpayer (any product, process, computer software, technique, formula, or invention to be held for sale, lease, or license, or used by the taxpayer in its trade or business) for a <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41d3-qualified-research-permitted-purposes\">qualified purpose</a>—specifically, a new or improved function, performance, reliability, or quality (expressly excluding style, taste, cosmetic, or seasonal design factors).",
