        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Assume a hypothetical mature domestic aerospace manufacturing corporation has a verified and consistent Fixed-Base Percentage (FBP) of <strong>8.00%</strong> based on its 1984–1988 aggregate QREs and gross receipts (well below the 16.00% statutory ceiling of 26 U.S.C. § 41(c)(3)(C)). In the current credit year, the corporation incurs <strong>$2,000,000</strong> in qualified research expenses (comprising in-house wages, supplies, and <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41b3-contract-research-expenses\">65% of eligible contract research</a> per IRC § 41(b)). Over the four preceding taxable years, its net gross receipts (after excluding returns, allowances, and capital asset sales per 26 CFR § 1.41-3(c)(2)) were <strong>$40,000,000</strong> (Year -1), <strong>$38,000,000</strong> (Year -2), <strong>$35,000,000</strong> (Year -3), and <strong>$31,000,000</strong> (Year -4)."
