        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "This nominal 10.0 percent marginal rate is then reduced a second time by <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-280cc-rd-credit-deduction-disallowance\">26 U.S.C. § 280C(c)</a>, which prevents taxpayers from claiming both a full research expense deduction (or capital account charge) and a full tax credit on the same expenditure dollar. Under <a href=\"/us/faq/does-rd-tax-credit-reduce-tax-basis-section-280c\">26 U.S.C. § 280C(c)(1)</a> (as amended by Pub. L. 119-21, § 70302(b)(2)(B), effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024), a taxpayer's domestic research or experimental expenditures under § 174A(b) otherwise taken into account as a deduction or charged to capital account must be reduced by 100 percent of the research credit allowed under § 41(a). Alternatively, under 26 U.S.C. § 280C(c)(2), a taxpayer may make an irrevocable election on its timely filed original return (including extensions, via <a href=\"/us/glossary/irs-form-6765\">IRS Form 6765</a>) to avoid reducing its deduction and instead claim a reduced credit equal to the § 41(a) credit minus the product of that credit and the maximum corporate tax rate under 26 U.S.C. § 11(b)."
