        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Even where a discrete business component theoretically satisfies the four-part qualification test of 26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(1), <a href=\"/us/legislation/26-usc-41d4-rd-tax-credit-excluded-activities\">26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(4)</a> establishes categorical exclusions that disqualify associated expenditures from the research tax credit. First, under the <a href=\"/us/glossary/adaptation-exclusion\">adaptation exclusion</a> of 26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(4)(B), any research activity related to the adaptation of an existing business component to a particular customer's requirement or need is strictly disqualified. When a company takes an existing software product or mechanical system and merely customizes the interface or integrates it into a client's specific server or operational environment using known coding or engineering parameters, the activity is statutorily excluded from qualified research."
