      {
        "type": "heading",
        "text": "Analytical Synthesis and Compliance Conclusions"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "The Business Component Test under 26 U.S.C. § 41(d)(2) and its implementing shrinking-back regulation under 26 C.F.R. § 1.41-4(b)(2) serve as the uncompromising structural foundation of the federal and state R&D tax credit framework, prohibiting the blending of routine commercial development with genuine technological innovation. Defining a business component at the macro-project level maximizes the potential pool of QREs—capturing incidental supplies and integration labor when non-experimental activities remain below 20%, as demonstrated by the two qualifying prototype vessels in <em>Trinity Industries</em>—but exposes the taxpayer to catastrophic cliff-edge disallowance whenever routine activities exceed 20%."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "As confirmed across federal and state tribunals in <em>Little Sandy Coal</em>, <em>Trinity Industries</em>, <em>Davenport</em>, <em>Betz</em>, <em>Phoenix Design Group</em>, <em>Tell City Boatworks</em>, <em>Appeal of Walden</em>, <em>Appeal of Abramson</em>, and Idaho Docket No. 1-707-713-536, the shrinking-back rule cannot be invoked as a post-hoc legal argument during an audit or litigation unless the taxpayer's underlying project accounting already segments labor and supply costs by sub-system and sub-assembly. To secure and defend research tax credits across federal and state jurisdictions, modern enterprises must embed tax-compliance tracking directly into their engineering and scientific workflows, proactively identifying the discrete locus of technical uncertainty at the outset of a project, segmenting business components accordingly, and generating <a href=\"/us/faq/contemporaneous-documentation-rd-tax-credit\">contemporaneous documentation</a> of the iterative trial-and-error process."
      }
