        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Although unused California R&D tax credits <a href=\"/us/glossary/research-credit-carryforward\">carry forward</a> indefinitely until exhausted (and cannot be carried back), state budget legislation enacted in 2024—specifically <strong>Senate Bill 167 (SB 167)</strong> and <strong>Senate Bill 175 (SB 175, Chapter 42, Statutes of 2024)</strong>, codified at <strong>R&TC Section 23036.4</strong> (Corporation Tax Law) and <strong>R&TC Section 17039.4</strong> (Personal Income Tax Law)—enacted a temporary business credit utilization limitation for taxable years beginning on or after <strong>January 1, 2024, and before January 1, 2027</strong>. During this three-year window, business tax credits—including current and carryforward R&D credits—cannot reduce net tax (for personal income filers) or tax (for corporate filers) by more than <strong>$5,000,000</strong> per taxable year. Crucially for multi-entity structures, under R&TC Section 23036.4 and the Form FTB 3523 Instructions, where taxpayers are included in a unitary <strong>combined report</strong>, this $5,000,000 annual utilization cap is enforced strictly at the <strong>aggregate group level</strong>, rather than on a separate per-entity basis, preventing combined reporting groups from multiplying the $5,000,000 cap across affiliated subsidiaries. Under SB 175 and the Form FTB 3523 Instructions, taxpayers (other than S-corporations with respect to entity-level credits) may make an annual, irrevocable all-or-nothing election on <strong>Form FTB 3870</strong> attached to an original, timely filed return to receive an annual refundable credit for business credits disallowed specifically due to the $5,000,000 limitation, claimed at <strong>20 percent</strong> per year over a five-year refundable period beginning in the third taxable year after the election year; if no refundable election is made, the carryover period for disallowed credits is extended by the number of taxable years the credit was disallowed."
